11:57:43 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['European Union law\n1. What do the Treaties and Regulations ensure about their effect?', 'European Union law\nAnswer 1: The Treaties and Regulations ensure that they have direct effect if they are clear, unconditional, and immediate.', 'European Union law\nQuestion 2: 2. Do Directives give citizens the standing to sue other citizens?', "European Union law\nAnswer 2: No, Directives do not generally give citizens the standing to sue other citizens, even though they are meant to protect citizens' rights.", 'European Union law\nQuestion 3: 3. What are the reasons cited by the Court of Justice for not granting standing to citizens for Directives?', 'European Union law\nAnswer 3: The Court of Justice believes that Directives are addressed to member states, and leave the choice of form and methods to implement them to the national authorities. Moreover, Directives often create minimum standards, allowing member states to apply higher standards.', 'European Union law\nQuestion 4: 4. Can citizens use Directives as a basis for making claims against other citizens in national law?', 'European Union law\nAnswer 4: Yes, citizens can use national laws that implement Directives as the basis for making claims against other citizens in national law.', 'European Union law\nQuestion 5: 5. How do the exceptions to the "horizontal" direct effect of Directives affect citizens\' rights?', 'European Union law\nAnswer 5: There are five large exceptions that have been adopted, allowing citizens to exercise rights guaranteed by Directives through legal action in certain circumstances.', 'European Union law\nYou are an AI assistant. User will you give you a task. Your goal is to complete the task as faithfully as you can. While performing the task think step-by-step and justify your steps.', 'European Union law\nWhile the Treaties and Regulations will have direct effect (if clear, unconditional and immediate), Directives do not generally give citizens (as opposed to the member state) standing to sue other citizens. In theory, this is because TFEU article 288 says Directives are addressed to the member states and usually "leave to the national authorities the choice of form and methods" to implement. In part this reflects that directives often create minimum standards, leaving member states to apply higher standards. For example, the Working Time Directive requires that every worker has at least 4 weeks paid holidays each year, but most member states require more than 28 days in national law. However, on the current position adopted by the Court of Justice, citizens have standing to make claims based on national laws that implement Directives, but not from Directives themselves. Directives do not have so called "horizontal" direct effect (i.e. between non-state parties). This view was instantly controversial, and in the early 1990s three Advocate Generals persuasively argued that Directives should create rights and duties for all citizens. The Court of Justice refused, but there are five large exceptions.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'European Union law', 'context': 'While the Treaties and Regulations will have direct effect (if clear, unconditional and immediate), Directives do not generally give citizens (as opposed to the member state) standing to sue other citizens. In theory, this is because TFEU article 288 says Directives are addressed to the member states and usually "leave to the national authorities the choice of form and methods" to implement. In part this reflects that directives often create minimum standards, leaving member states to apply higher standards. For example, the Working Time Directive requires that every worker has at least 4 weeks paid holidays each year, but most member states require more than 28 days in national law. However, on the current position adopted by the Court of Justice, citizens have standing to make claims based on national laws that implement Directives, but not from Directives themselves. Directives do not have so called "horizontal" direct effect (i.e. between non-state parties). This view was instantly controversial, and in the early 1990s three Advocate Generals persuasively argued that Directives should create rights and duties for all citizens. The Court of Justice refused, but there are five large exceptions.', 'question': 'Topic: European Union law\nWhat generally does not allow citizens to sue other citizens?', 'answer': 'Directives'}, {'title': 'European Union law', 'context': 'While the Treaties and Regulations will have direct effect (if clear, unconditional and immediate), Directives do not generally give citizens (as opposed to the member state) standing to sue other citizens. In theory, this is because TFEU article 288 says Directives are addressed to the member states and usually "leave to the national authorities the choice of form and methods" to implement. In part this reflects that directives often create minimum standards, leaving member states to apply higher standards. For example, the Working Time Directive requires that every worker has at least 4 weeks paid holidays each year, but most member states require more than 28 days in national law. However, on the current position adopted by the Court of Justice, citizens have standing to make claims based on national laws that implement Directives, but not from Directives themselves. Directives do not have so called "horizontal" direct effect (i.e. between non-state parties). This view was instantly controversial, and in the early 1990s three Advocate Generals persuasively argued that Directives should create rights and duties for all citizens. The Court of Justice refused, but there are five large exceptions.', 'question': 'Topic: European Union law\nHow many paid holiday days does the Working Time directive require workers to have each year?', 'answer': '4 weeks'}, {'title': 'European Union law', 'context': 'While the Treaties and Regulations will have direct effect (if clear, unconditional and immediate), Directives do not generally give citizens (as opposed to the member state) standing to sue other citizens. In theory, this is because TFEU article 288 says Directives are addressed to the member states and usually "leave to the national authorities the choice of form and methods" to implement. In part this reflects that directives often create minimum standards, leaving member states to apply higher standards. For example, the Working Time Directive requires that every worker has at least 4 weeks paid holidays each year, but most member states require more than 28 days in national law. However, on the current position adopted by the Court of Justice, citizens have standing to make claims based on national laws that implement Directives, but not from Directives themselves. Directives do not have so called "horizontal" direct effect (i.e. between non-state parties). This view was instantly controversial, and in the early 1990s three Advocate Generals persuasively argued that Directives should create rights and duties for all citizens. The Court of Justice refused, but there are five large exceptions.', 'question': 'Topic: European Union law\nHow many paid holiday days do most member states require?', 'answer': '28 days'}, {'title': 'European Union law', 'context': 'While the Treaties and Regulations will have direct effect (if clear, unconditional and immediate), Directives do not generally give citizens (as opposed to the member state) standing to sue other citizens. In theory, this is because TFEU article 288 says Directives are addressed to the member states and usually "leave to the national authorities the choice of form and methods" to implement. In part this reflects that directives often create minimum standards, leaving member states to apply higher standards. For example, the Working Time Directive requires that every worker has at least 4 weeks paid holidays each year, but most member states require more than 28 days in national law. However, on the current position adopted by the Court of Justice, citizens have standing to make claims based on national laws that implement Directives, but not from Directives themselves. Directives do not have so called "horizontal" direct effect (i.e. between non-state parties). This view was instantly controversial, and in the early 1990s three Advocate Generals persuasively argued that Directives should create rights and duties for all citizens. The Court of Justice refused, but there are five large exceptions.', 'question': 'Topic: European Union law\nWhen did the three Advocate Generals argue that Directives should create rights and duties for all citizens?', 'answer': 'early 1990s'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
