12:17:11 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Martin Luther\nWhat text did Martin Luther translate?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 1: The Bible.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 2:  In what language did Martin Luther translate the Bible?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 2: German.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 3:  When did Martin Luther complete his translation of the Old Testament?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 3: 1534.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 4:  How many years did Martin Luther spend refining his translation of the Bible?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 4: He continued to refine it until the end of his life, but no specific number of years is mentioned.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 5:  What passage of the Bible did Luther translate?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 5: Romans 3:28.', "Martin Luther\nQuestion 6:  What part of Martin Luther's doctrine did his translation of Romans 3:28 emphasize?", 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 6: That we are justified by faith in Christ without any works of the Law.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 7:  How did Martin Luther reply when he was criticized for adding "alone" after "faith" in Romans 3:28?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 7: He replied that the text itself and the meaning of St. Paul urgently require and demand it.', "Martin Luther\nQuestion 8:  What is the main point of Christian doctrine that Martin Luther's translation emphasized?", 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 8: That we are justified by faith in Christ without any works of the Law.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 9:  What did Martin Luther say we must say when speaking about the cutting away of works?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 9: "Faith alone justifies us, and not works."', 'Martin Luther\nYou are an AI assistant. You will be given a task. You must generate a detailed and long answer.', 'Martin Luther\nLuther had published his German translation of the New Testament in 1522, and he and his collaborators completed the translation of the Old Testament in 1534, when the whole Bible was published. He continued to work on refining the translation until the end of his life. Others had translated the Bible into German, but Luther tailored his translation to his own doctrine. When he was criticised for inserting the word "alone" after "faith" in Romans 3:28, he replied in part: "[T]he text itself and the meaning of St. Paul urgently require and demand it. For in that very passage he is dealing with the main point of Christian doctrine, namely, that we are justified by faith in Christ without any works of the Law. ... But when works are so completely cut away – and that must mean that faith alone justifies – whoever would speak plainly and clearly about this cutting away of works will have to say, \'Faith alone justifies us, and not works\'."'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'Luther had published his German translation of the New Testament in 1522, and he and his collaborators completed the translation of the Old Testament in 1534, when the whole Bible was published. He continued to work on refining the translation until the end of his life. Others had translated the Bible into German, but Luther tailored his translation to his own doctrine. When he was criticised for inserting the word "alone" after "faith" in Romans 3:28, he replied in part: "[T]he text itself and the meaning of St. Paul urgently require and demand it. For in that very passage he is dealing with the main point of Christian doctrine, namely, that we are justified by faith in Christ without any works of the Law. ... But when works are so completely cut away – and that must mean that faith alone justifies – whoever would speak plainly and clearly about this cutting away of works will have to say, \'Faith alone justifies us, and not works\'."', 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhen did Martin Luther publish his translation of the New Testament?', 'answer': '1522'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'Luther had published his German translation of the New Testament in 1522, and he and his collaborators completed the translation of the Old Testament in 1534, when the whole Bible was published. He continued to work on refining the translation until the end of his life. Others had translated the Bible into German, but Luther tailored his translation to his own doctrine. When he was criticised for inserting the word "alone" after "faith" in Romans 3:28, he replied in part: "[T]he text itself and the meaning of St. Paul urgently require and demand it. For in that very passage he is dealing with the main point of Christian doctrine, namely, that we are justified by faith in Christ without any works of the Law. ... But when works are so completely cut away – and that must mean that faith alone justifies – whoever would speak plainly and clearly about this cutting away of works will have to say, \'Faith alone justifies us, and not works\'."', 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhen was the Old Testament translation finished?', 'answer': '1534'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'Luther had published his German translation of the New Testament in 1522, and he and his collaborators completed the translation of the Old Testament in 1534, when the whole Bible was published. He continued to work on refining the translation until the end of his life. Others had translated the Bible into German, but Luther tailored his translation to his own doctrine. When he was criticised for inserting the word "alone" after "faith" in Romans 3:28, he replied in part: "[T]he text itself and the meaning of St. Paul urgently require and demand it. For in that very passage he is dealing with the main point of Christian doctrine, namely, that we are justified by faith in Christ without any works of the Law. ... But when works are so completely cut away – and that must mean that faith alone justifies – whoever would speak plainly and clearly about this cutting away of works will have to say, \'Faith alone justifies us, and not works\'."', 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat did he work on refining until the end of his life?', 'answer': 'the translation'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'Luther had published his German translation of the New Testament in 1522, and he and his collaborators completed the translation of the Old Testament in 1534, when the whole Bible was published. He continued to work on refining the translation until the end of his life. Others had translated the Bible into German, but Luther tailored his translation to his own doctrine. When he was criticised for inserting the word "alone" after "faith" in Romans 3:28, he replied in part: "[T]he text itself and the meaning of St. Paul urgently require and demand it. For in that very passage he is dealing with the main point of Christian doctrine, namely, that we are justified by faith in Christ without any works of the Law. ... But when works are so completely cut away – and that must mean that faith alone justifies – whoever would speak plainly and clearly about this cutting away of works will have to say, \'Faith alone justifies us, and not works\'."', 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat word was Luther criticized for adding in Romans3:28?', 'answer': 'alone'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'Luther had published his German translation of the New Testament in 1522, and he and his collaborators completed the translation of the Old Testament in 1534, when the whole Bible was published. He continued to work on refining the translation until the end of his life. Others had translated the Bible into German, but Luther tailored his translation to his own doctrine. When he was criticised for inserting the word "alone" after "faith" in Romans 3:28, he replied in part: "[T]he text itself and the meaning of St. Paul urgently require and demand it. For in that very passage he is dealing with the main point of Christian doctrine, namely, that we are justified by faith in Christ without any works of the Law. ... But when works are so completely cut away – and that must mean that faith alone justifies – whoever would speak plainly and clearly about this cutting away of works will have to say, \'Faith alone justifies us, and not works\'."', 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nAccording to Luther what justifies us?', 'answer': 'Faith alone'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
