12:20:02 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Doctor Who\nWhy was the concept of regeneration introduced for the main character of Doctor Who?', "Doctor Who\nAnswer 1: The concept of regeneration was introduced to permit the recasting of the main character, Doctor Who, which allowed for the depiction of alternative Doctors from the Doctor's relative past or future and facilitated the show's history's continuation.", 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 2: Who first introduced the idea of regeneration for Doctor Who?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 2: The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration, although the Second Doctor had merely described undergoing a "change of appearance" and William Hartnell\'s Doctor underwent a "renewal" due to poor health.', "Doctor Who\nQuestion 3: How does the regeneration concept contribute to the Doctor Who show's story?", "Doctor Who\nAnswer 3: The regeneration concept allows for the depiction of alternative Doctors from the Doctor's relative past or future, which is crucial for the show's story as it helps portray different versions or incarnations of the main character across the series.", 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 4: Why was regeneration necessary for the main character in the early stages of Doctor Who?', "Doctor Who\nAnswer 4: Regeneration was necessary for the main character in the early stages of Doctor Who due to the recasting of the actor and the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor's relative past or future. This helped overcome the challenge of changing actors for the role of the main character without breaking the continuity of the show.", 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 5: What prompted the original star of Doctor Who, William Hartnell, to leave the show?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 5: The original star of Doctor Who, William Hartnell, left the show due to his poor health, which triggered the need for the introduction of the regeneration concept to continue the story without losing the main character.', 'Doctor Who\nProducers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'Producers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Doctor Who\nWhat term is used to explain a change in the appearance of Doctor Who?', 'answer': 'regeneration'}, {'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'Producers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Doctor Who\nWhen was the term "regeneration" first used?', 'answer': "the Doctor's third on-screen regeneration"}, {'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'Producers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Doctor Who\nWhat first prompted the regeneration concept?', 'answer': "William Hartnell's poor health"}, {'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'Producers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Doctor Who\nWhat term was used for the first regeneration?', 'answer': 'renewal'}, {'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'Producers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Doctor Who\nWhat term was used for the second regeneration?', 'answer': 'change of appearance'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
