12:22:24 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Victoria and Albert Museum\nQuestion 1: When was the Victoria and Albert Museum established? \n\nAnswer:  The Victoria and Albert Museum was established in 1852.', 'Victoria and Albert Museum\nQuestion 2:  Who did the museum get its name from?\n\nAnswer:  The museum got its name from Queen Victoria and Prince Albert.\n', 'Victoria and Albert Museum\nQuestion 3:  Where is the Victoria and Albert Museum located?\n\nAnswer:  The Victoria and Albert Museum is located in the Brompton district of the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea in London.\n', 'Victoria and Albert Museum\nQuestion 4:  What is the significance of the area in which the V&A is located, also known as "Albertopolis"?\n\nAnswer:  The area, known as "Albertopolis," is significant because of its association with Prince Albert, the Albert Memorial, and major cultural institutions with which he was associated, such as the Natural History Museum, the Science Museum, and the Royal Albert Hall.\n', "Victoria and Albert Museum\nQuestion 5:  What is the V&A's status as a government organization?\n\nAnswer:  The V&A is a non-departmental public body sponsored by the Department for Culture, Media and Sport.\n", "Victoria and Albert Museum\nQuestion 6:  How has the public's access to the Victoria and Albert Museum been affected in recent years?\n\nAnswer:  Since 2001, like other national British museums, entrance to the Victoria and Albert Museum has been free.\nYou are an AI assistant. User will you give you a task. Your goal is to complete the task as faithfully as you can. While performing the task think step-by-step and justify your steps.", 'Victoria and Albert Museum\nThe Victoria and Albert Museum (often abbreviated as the V&A), London, is the world\'s largest museum of decorative arts and design, housing a permanent collection of over 4.5 million objects. It was founded in 1852 and named after Queen Victoria and Prince Albert. The V&A is located in the Brompton district of the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea, in an area that has become known as "Albertopolis" because of its association with Prince Albert, the Albert Memorial and the major cultural institutions with which he was associated. These include the Natural History Museum, the Science Museum and the Royal Albert Hall. The museum is a non-departmental public body sponsored by the Department for Culture, Media and Sport. Like other national British museums, entrance to the museum has been free since 2001.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Victoria and Albert Museum', 'context': 'The Victoria and Albert Museum (often abbreviated as the V&A), London, is the world\'s largest museum of decorative arts and design, housing a permanent collection of over 4.5 million objects. It was founded in 1852 and named after Queen Victoria and Prince Albert. The V&A is located in the Brompton district of the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea, in an area that has become known as "Albertopolis" because of its association with Prince Albert, the Albert Memorial and the major cultural institutions with which he was associated. These include the Natural History Museum, the Science Museum and the Royal Albert Hall. The museum is a non-departmental public body sponsored by the Department for Culture, Media and Sport. Like other national British museums, entrance to the museum has been free since 2001.', 'question': 'Topic: Victoria and Albert Museum\nwhere is the Victoria and Albert Museum located?', 'answer': 'The V&A is located in the Brompton district of the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea'}, {'title': 'Victoria and Albert Museum', 'context': 'The Victoria and Albert Museum (often abbreviated as the V&A), London, is the world\'s largest museum of decorative arts and design, housing a permanent collection of over 4.5 million objects. It was founded in 1852 and named after Queen Victoria and Prince Albert. The V&A is located in the Brompton district of the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea, in an area that has become known as "Albertopolis" because of its association with Prince Albert, the Albert Memorial and the major cultural institutions with which he was associated. These include the Natural History Museum, the Science Museum and the Royal Albert Hall. The museum is a non-departmental public body sponsored by the Department for Culture, Media and Sport. Like other national British museums, entrance to the museum has been free since 2001.', 'question': 'Topic: Victoria and Albert Museum\nhow many permanent objects are located there?', 'answer': 'a permanent collection of over 4.5 million objects.'}, {'title': 'Victoria and Albert Museum', 'context': 'The Victoria and Albert Museum (often abbreviated as the V&A), London, is the world\'s largest museum of decorative arts and design, housing a permanent collection of over 4.5 million objects. It was founded in 1852 and named after Queen Victoria and Prince Albert. The V&A is located in the Brompton district of the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea, in an area that has become known as "Albertopolis" because of its association with Prince Albert, the Albert Memorial and the major cultural institutions with which he was associated. These include the Natural History Museum, the Science Museum and the Royal Albert Hall. The museum is a non-departmental public body sponsored by the Department for Culture, Media and Sport. Like other national British museums, entrance to the museum has been free since 2001.', 'question': 'Topic: Victoria and Albert Museum\nwhen was the Victoria and Albert museum founded?', 'answer': 'It was founded in 1852'}, {'title': 'Victoria and Albert Museum', 'context': 'The Victoria and Albert Museum (often abbreviated as the V&A), London, is the world\'s largest museum of decorative arts and design, housing a permanent collection of over 4.5 million objects. It was founded in 1852 and named after Queen Victoria and Prince Albert. T