12:22:53 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Private school\nWhat is a private school?', 'Private school\nAnswer 1: A private school is a type of education institution that is privately funded and operated, typically distinct from public schools that receive funding and direction from the government. Examples of private schools include those established by missionaries in the early nineteenth century in South Africa and other church schools as well as schools that are privately governed.', 'Private school\nQuestion 2: What happened to the private sector after the abolition of apartheid?', 'Private school\nAnswer 2: After the abolition of apartheid in South Africa, the laws governing private education changed significantly. The private sector continued to grow, and the South African Schools Act of 1996 established two categories of schools: "public" (state-controlled) and "independent" (which includes traditional private schools and schools which are privately governed[clarification needed]).', 'Private school\nRewrite this passage in your own words.', 'Private school\nAfter the end of apartheid, the laws about private schools changed in South Africa. They now have two types of schools: public, which are run by the government, and independent, which can be private schools like the ones set up by missionaries or schools run by private companies. The South African Schools Act of 1996 is the law that recognizes these two types of schools.', 'Private school\nDefinition of terms', 'Private school\nAutonomous school', "Private school\nAccording to the provisions of the South African Schools Act, an autonomous school is a designated independent school that has the status of an independent school and is subject to the regulations prescribed in the national norms and standards for autonomous schools. The self-governance of an autonomous school is limited only by the statute of the school and the national norms and standards for autonomous schools. As with any other independent school, an autonomous school derives its powers from the national norms and standards for independent schools and the conditions in the school's statute. The responsibilities, decision-making, management and administration of the school are determined by its board of governance and the principal in accordance with its statute and the national norms and standards for independent schools.", 'Private school\nAutonomous schools should not be confused with non-profit organisation schools. A non-profit organisation (NPO) is a legal entity that does not distribute its earnings to its owners, members or shareholders. Non-profit organisation schools are registered as NPOs, but not all non-profit organisation schools are autonomous schools. NPOs register with the Department of Social Development or as a public benefit company with the Companies and Intellectual Property Commission. An autonomous school can be an NPO, but the requirements to be an NPO are separate from the requirements to be an autonomous school. The Act also makes provisions for a so-called emerging autonomous school to be approved by the department and for a fee not exceeding the applicable registration fee to be paid to the department.', "Private school\nAutonomous schools cannot register as NPOs or Public Benefit Companies (PBC), the reason behind this being that they are not a group of 2 or more people who work together, so they can’t register as PBC, as that needs 2 or more people, as well as each person being at least 18 years old. Autonomous schools can apply for the status of an NPO if they meet the requirements, but that's a separate process from becoming an autonomous school.", 'Private school\nQuestion 3: What is an autonomous school according to the South African Schools Act?', "Private school\nAnswer 3: An autonomous school, according to the South African Schools Act, is an independent school that is designated as such and is subject to specific regulations. These regulations are outlined in the national norms and standards for autonomous schools. Autonomous schools are self-governed within these established norms and standards, but their governance and administration are still bound by their own statutes and the conditions outlined by the national norms and standards for independent schools. The responsibilities, decision-making, management, and administration of an autonomous school are the purview of its board of governance and principal, who function according to the school's statute and the national norms and standards for independent schools.", 'Private school\nQuestion 4: Can autonomous schools be confused with non-profit organization (NPO) schools?', 'Private school\nAnswer 4: Yes, autonomous schools can be confused with non-profit organization (NPO) schools because both types of schools may exist independently of public schools and function similarly in their operations and funding. However, they should not be confused as they serve different purposes. An autonomous school is a designated independent school with specific legal status and requirements, while an NPO school is a school that is registered as an NPO. These schools are registered with the Department of Social Development or as a Public Benefit Company (PBC) with the Companies and Intellectual Property Commission. While an autonomous school can theoretically be an NPO if it meets the criteria, these requirements are separate from the criteria for being an autonomous school.', "Private school\nQuestion 5: Why can't autonomous schools register as NPOs or Public Benefit Companies (PBCs)?", 'Private school\nAnswer 5: Autonomous schools are unable to register as NPOs or Public Benefit Companies (PBCs) primarily because they are not a group of people coming together to form a legal entity. NPO registration and PBC registration each have their own legal requirements that autonomous schools do not fulfill. Specifically, NPO registration in South Africa requires that the organization must be a group of two or more people who work together, and each person must be at least 18 years old. Since autonomous schools are already autonomous entities that operate independently, they do not qualify for NPO registration based on these criteria. However, autonomous schools may choose to apply for NPO status if they meet the necessary criteria for registration as an NPO, but this decision is separate from their status as an autonomous school.', 'Private school\nSome of the oldest schools in South Africa are private church schools that were established by missionaries in the early nineteenth century. The private sector has grown ever since. After the abolition of apartheid, the laws governing private education in South Africa changed significantly. The South African Schools Act of 1996 recognises two categories of schools: "public" (state-controlled) and "independent" (which includes traditional private schools and schools which are privately governed[clarification needed].)'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Private school', 'context': 'Some of the oldest schools in South Africa are private church schools that were established by missionaries in the early nineteenth century. The private sector has grown ever since. After the abolition of apartheid, the laws governing private education in South Africa changed significantly. The South African Schools Act of 1996 recognises two categories of schools: "public" (state-controlled) and "independent" (which includes traditional private schools and schools which are privately governed[clarification needed].)', 'question': 'Topic: Private school\nWhat South African law recognized two types of schools?', 'answer': 'South African Schools Act'}, {'title': 'Private school', 'context': 'Some of the oldest schools in South Africa are private church schools that were established by missionaries in the early nineteenth century. The private sector has grown ever since. After the abolition of apartheid, the laws governing private education in South Africa changed significantly. The South African Schools Act of 1996 recognises two categories of schools: "public" (state-controlled) and "independent" (which includes traditional private schools and schools which are privately governed[clarification needed].)', 'question': 'Topic: Private school\nIn what year was the South African Schools Act passed?', 'answer': '1996'}, {'title': 'Private school', 'context': 'Some of the oldest schools in South Africa are private church schools that were established by missionaries in the early nineteenth century. The private sector has grown ever since. After the abolition of apartheid, the laws governing private education in South Africa changed significantly. The South African Schools Act of 1996 recognises two categories of schools: "public" (state-controlled) and "independent" (which includes traditional private schools and schools which are privately governed[clarification needed].)', 'question': 'Topic: Private school\nAlong with public schools, what type of school was recognized under the South African Schools Act?', 'answer': 'independent'}, {'title': 'Private school', 'context': 'Some of the oldest schools in South Africa are private church schools that were established by missionaries in the early nineteenth century. The private sector has grown ever since. After the abolition of apartheid, the laws governing private education in South Africa changed significantly. The South African Schools Act of 1996 recognises two categories of schools: "public" (state-controlled) and "independent" (which includes traditional private schools and schools which are privately governed[clarification needed].)', 'question': 'Topic: Private school\nIn South Africa, along with privately governed schools, what schools are classified as independent?', 'answer': 'traditional private'}, {'title': 'Private school', 'context': 'Some of the oldest schools in South Africa are private church schools that were established by missionaries in the early nineteenth century. The private sector has grown ever since. After the abolition of apartheid, the laws governing private education in South Africa changed significantly. The South African Schools Act of 1996 recognises two categories of schools: "public" (state-controlled) and "independent" (which includes traditional private schools and schools which are privately governed[clarification needed].)', 'question': 'Topic: Private school\nIn what century did missionaries notably establish church schools in South Africa?', 'answer': 'nineteenth'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
