12:23:45 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Computational complexity theory\nCan a Cobham-Edmonds thesis give more than one polynomial function time?', 'Computational complexity theory\nAnswer 1:  No, a Cobham-Edmonds thesis can only give one polynomial function time in any two reasonable and general models of computation.', 'Computational complexity theory\nQuestion 2:  What are Cobham-Edmonds thesis related to?', 'Computational complexity theory\nAnswer 2:  Cobham-Edmonds thesis is related to the complexity class P, which is the set of decision problems solvable by a deterministic Turing machine within polynomial time.', 'Computational complexity theory\nQuestion 3:  What is FP?', 'Computational complexity theory\nAnswer 3:  FP is the corresponding set of function problems to the complexity class P.', "Computational complexity theory\nNow let's discuss the computational complexity theory and the language of binary strings in a more formal way.", 'Computational complexity theory\nFormal discussion:', 'Computational complexity theory\nIn computational complexity theory, the Cobham-Edmonds thesis states that the time complexities in any two reasonable and general models of computation are polynomially related. This means that the running time of an algorithm can be described using a polynomial function of the input size, regardless of the specific computational model used.', 'Computational complexity theory\nFor example, consider the language {xx | x is any binary string}. This language can be solved in linear time on a multi-tape Turing machine, meaning the running time is O(n), where n is the length of the input string. However, the same language necessarily requires quadratic time in the model of single-tape Turing machines, with a running time of O(n^2). This shows that the complexity of an algorithm can depend on the chosen machine model.', 'Computational complexity theory\nThe complexity class P, which stands for "polynomial time," is the set of decision problems that can be solved by a deterministic Turing machine within polynomial time. In other words, P is the set of problems that can be solved efficiently, where the running time of the algorithm is bounded by a polynomial function of the input size.', 'Computational complexity theory\nThe corresponding set of function problems is FP, which is the set of problems that can be solved by a deterministic Turing machine within polynomial time using a function as the output. In the case of the language {xx | x is any binary string}, the function output would be the string xx.', 'Computational complexity theory\nOverall, computational complexity theory is concerned with understanding the inherent difficulty of solving computational problems and how different computational models affect the complexity of those problems. The Cobham-Edmonds thesis provides a way to compare the complexity of algorithms across different models and sets the foundation for the complexity classes P and FP.', 'Computational complexity theory\nIn terms of the computational complexity theory in class P and FP, how might a neural network fit into this discussion?', 'Computational complexity theory\nBut bounding the computation time above by some concrete function f(n) often yields complexity classes that depend on the chosen machine model. For instance, the language {xx | x is any binary string} can be solved in linear time on a multi-tape Turing machine, but necessarily requires quadratic time in the model of single-tape Turing machines. If we allow polynomial variations in running time, Cobham-Edmonds thesis states that "the time complexities in any two reasonable and general models of computation are polynomially related" (Goldreich 2008, Chapter 1.2). This forms the basis for the complexity class P, which is the set of decision problems solvable by a deterministic Turing machine within polynomial time. The corresponding set of function problems is FP.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Computational complexity theory', 'context': 'But bounding the computation time above by some concrete function f(n) often yields complexity classes that depend on the chosen machine model. For instance, the language {xx | x is any binary string} can be solved in linear time on a multi-tape Turing machine, but necessarily requires quadratic time in the model of single-tape Turing machines. If we allow polynomial variations in running time, Cobham-Edmonds thesis states that "the time complexities in any two reasonable and general models of computation are polynomially related" (Goldreich 2008, Chapter 1.2). This forms the basis for the complexity class P, which is the set of decision problems solvable by a deterministic Turing machine within polynomial time. The corresponding set of function problems is FP.', 'question': 'Topic: Computational complexity theory\nConcrete bounding of computation time frequently produces complexity classes contingent upon what?', 'answer': 'chosen machine model'}, {'title': 'Computational complexity theory', 'context': 'But bounding the computation time above by some concrete function f(n) often yields complexity classes that depend on the chosen machine model. For instance, the language {xx | x is any binary string} can be solved in linear time on a multi-tape Turing machine, but necessarily requires quadratic time in the model of single-tape Turing machines. If we allow polynomial variations in running time, Cobham-Edmonds thesis states that "the time complexities in any two reasonable and general models of computation are polynomially related" (Goldreich 2008, Chapter 1.2). This forms the basis for the complexity class P, which is the set of decision problems solvable by a deterministic Turing machine within polynomial time. The corresponding set of function problems is FP.', 'question': 'Topic: Computational complexity theory\nA multi-tape Turing machine requires what type of time for a solution?', 'answer': 'linear time'}, {'title': 'Computational complexity theory', 'context': 'But bounding the computation time above by some concrete function f(n) often yields complexity classes that depend on the chosen machine model. For instance, the language {xx | x is any binary string} can be solved in linear time on a multi-tape Turing machine, but necessarily requires quadratic time in the model of single-tape Turing machines. If we allow polynomial variations in running time, Cobham-Edmonds thesis states that "the time complexities in any two reasonable and general models of computation are polynomially related" (Goldreich 2008, Chapter 1.2). This forms the basis for the complexity class P, which is the set of decision problems solvable by a deterministic Turing machine within polynomial time. The corresponding set of function problems is FP.', 'question': 'Topic: Computational complexity theory\nA language solved in quadratic time implies the use of what type of Turing machine?', 'answer': 'single-tape Turing machines'}, {'title': 'Computational complexity theory', 'context': 'But bounding the computation time above by some concrete function f(n) often yields complexity classes that depend on the chosen machine model. For instance, the language {xx | x is any binary string} can be solved in linear time on a multi-tape Turing machine, but necessarily requires quadratic time in the model of single-tape Turing machines. If we allow polynomial variations in running time, Cobham-Edmonds thesis states that "the time complexities in any two reasonable and general models of computation are polynomially related" (Goldreich 2008, Chapter 1.2). This forms the basis for the complexity class P, which is the set of decision problems solvable by a deterministic Turing machine within polynomial time. The corresponding set of function problems is FP.', 'question': 'Topic: Computational complexity theory\nWhat thesis specifies that a polynomial relationship exists within time complexities in a computational model?', 'answer': 'Cobham-Edmonds thesis'}, {'title': 'Computational complexity theory', 'context': 'But bounding the computation time above by some concrete function f(n) often yields complexity classes that depend on the chosen machine model. For instance, the language {xx | x is any binary string} can be solved in linear time on a multi-tape Turing machine, but necessarily requires quadratic time in the model of single-tape Turing machines. If we allow polynomial variations in running time, Cobham-Edmonds thesis states that "the time complexities in any two reasonable and general models of computation are polynomially related" (Goldreich 2008, Chapter 1.2). This forms the basis for the complexity class P, which is the set of decision problems solvable by a deterministic Turing machine within polynomial time. The corresponding set of function problems is FP.', 'question': 'Topic: Computational complexity theory\nDecision problems capable of being solved by a deterministic Turing machine while maintaining adherence to polynomial time belong to what class?', 'answer': 'complexity class P'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
