12:24:26 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ["Martin Luther\nWithout Luther's backing for the uprising, many rebels laid down their weapons; others felt betrayed. Their defeat by the Swabian League at the Battle of Frankenhausen on 15 May 1525, followed by Müntzer's execution, brought the revolutionary stage of the Reformation to a close. Why did this bring the revolutionary stage to a close?", "Martin Luther\nAnswer 1: The revolutionary stage of the Reformation came to a close because without Martin Luther's support for the uprising, many rebels were left without guidance and, as a result, either laid down their weapons or felt betrayed. Additionally, their defeat by the Swabian League at the Battle of Frankenhausen, along with the execution of Müntzer, further weakened the revolutionary forces, ultimately leading to the end of this stage of the Reformation.", "Martin Luther\nQuestion 2:  Which movement provided a refuge for those who were radical after Luther's Reformation had flourished under the wing of secular powers?", "Martin Luther\nAnswer 2: After Luther's Reformation had flourished under the wing of the secular powers, those who remained radical sought refuge in the anabaptist movement and other religious movements. These groups continued to advocate for their beliefs and ideals, albeit with less of an impact than the initial revolutionary stage under Martin Luther.", "Martin Luther\nQuestion 3:  What was the impact of Martin Luther's Reformation on the secular powers?", "Martin Luther\nAnswer 3: Martin Luther's Reformation, which flourished under the wing of the secular powers, had a significant impact on those in positions of authority. The movement, which began as a protest against various aspects of the Catholic Church, gained the support of local rulers and became intertwined with secular politics. This not only allowed for the spread of Luther's ideas but also contributed to the overall weakening of the Catholic Church's influence in European politics.", "Martin Luther\nQuestion 4:  How did the Battle of Frankenhausen and Müntzer's execution affect the revolutionary forces during the Reformation?", 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 4: The Battle of Frankenhausen on 15 May 1525 and the subsequent execution of Müntzer dealt a severe blow to the revolutionary forces during the Reformation. These events led to a significant setback for the rebels, who either laid down their weapons or felt betrayed by the lack of continued support from Martin Luther. This diminished their ability to sustain the revolutionary momentum and effectively marked the end of the revolutionary stage of the Reformation.', "Martin Luther\nWithout Luther's backing for the uprising, many rebels laid down their weapons; others felt betrayed. Their defeat by the Swabian League at the Battle of Frankenhausen on 15 May 1525, followed by Müntzer's execution, brought the revolutionary stage of the Reformation to a close. Thereafter, radicalism found a refuge in the anabaptist movement and other religious movements, while Luther's Reformation flourished under the wing of the secular powers."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': "Without Luther's backing for the uprising, many rebels laid down their weapons; others felt betrayed. Their defeat by the Swabian League at the Battle of Frankenhausen on 15 May 1525, followed by Müntzer's execution, brought the revolutionary stage of the Reformation to a close. Thereafter, radicalism found a refuge in the anabaptist movement and other religious movements, while Luther's Reformation flourished under the wing of the secular powers.", 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat did Luther deny the rebels?', 'answer': 'backing for the uprising'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': "Without Luther's backing for the uprising, many rebels laid down their weapons; others felt betrayed. Their defeat by the Swabian League at the Battle of Frankenhausen on 15 May 1525, followed by Müntzer's execution, brought the revolutionary stage of the Reformation to a close. Thereafter, radicalism found a refuge in the anabaptist movement and other religious movements, while Luther's Reformation flourished under the wing of the secular powers.", 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWho defeated the rebels at the Battle of Frankenhausen?', 'answer': 'Swabian League'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': "Without Luther's backing for the uprising, many rebels laid down their weapons; others felt betrayed. Their defeat by the Swabian League at the Battle of Frankenhausen on 15 May 1525, followed by Müntzer's execution, brought the revolutionary stage of the Reformation to a close. Thereafter, radicalism found a refuge in the anabaptist movement and other religious movements, while Luther's Reformation flourished under the wing of the secular powers.", 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhen was the Battle of Frankenhausen?', 'answer': '15 May 1525'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': "Without Luther's backing for the uprising, many rebels laid down their weapons; others felt betrayed. Their defeat by the Swabian League at the Battle of Frankenhausen on 15 May 1525, followed by Müntzer's execution, brought the revolutionary stage of the Reformation to a close. Thereafter, radicalism found a refuge in the anabaptist movement and other religious movements, while Luther's Reformation flourished under the wing of the secular powers.", 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat event followed the battle?', 'answer': "Müntzer's execution"}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': "Without Luther's backing for the uprising, many rebels laid down their weapons; others felt betrayed. Their defeat by the Swabian League at the Battle of Frankenhausen on 15 May 1525, followed by Müntzer's execution, brought the revolutionary stage of the Reformation to a close. Thereafter, radicalism found a refuge in the anabaptist movement and other religious movements, while Luther's Reformation 