12:29:11 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['American Broadcasting Company\nWho created the current ABC logo?', 'American Broadcasting Company\nAnswer 1: Paul Rand created the current ABC logo.', 'American Broadcasting Company\nQuestion 2:  When was the current ABC logo created?', 'American Broadcasting Company\nAnswer 2: The current ABC logo was created in 1962.', 'American Broadcasting Company\nQuestion 3:  What is a design aspect of the current ABC logo?', 'American Broadcasting Company\nAnswer 3: The current ABC logo has lowercase letters "abc" enclosed in a single black circle.', 'American Broadcasting Company\nQuestion 4:  What is an association between the current ABC logo and any typeface?', 'American Broadcasting Company\nAnswer 4: The current ABC logo is strongly reminiscent of the Bauhaus typeface designed by Herbert Bayer in the 1920s, and shares similarities with other fonts such as ITC Avant Garde and Horatio.', 'American Broadcasting Company\nQuestion 5:  What was a benefit of the redesign and duplication of the ABC logo?', 'American Broadcasting Company\nAnswer 5: The benefit of the redesign and duplication of the ABC logo was that it made it easier to redesign and duplicate before the advent of computer graphics.', 'American Broadcasting Company\nIn 1962, graphic designer Paul Rand redesigned the ABC logo into its best-known (and current) form, with the lowercase letters "abc" enclosed in a single black circle. The new logo debuted on-air for ABC\'s promos at the start of the 1963–64 season. The letters are strongly reminiscent of the Bauhaus typeface designed by Herbert Bayer in the 1920s, but also share similarities with several other fonts, such as ITC Avant Garde and Horatio, and most closely resembling Chalet. The logo\'s simplicity made it easier to redesign and duplicate, which conferred a benefit for ABC (mostly before the advent of computer graphics).'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'American Broadcasting Company', 'context': 'In 1962, graphic designer Paul Rand redesigned the ABC logo into its best-known (and current) form, with the lowercase letters "abc" enclosed in a single black circle. The new logo debuted on-air for ABC\'s promos at the start of the 1963–64 season. The letters are strongly reminiscent of the Bauhaus typeface designed by Herbert Bayer in the 1920s, but also share similarities with several other fonts, such as ITC Avant Garde and Horatio, and most closely resembling Chalet. The logo\'s simplicity made it easier to redesign and duplicate, which conferred a benefit for ABC (mostly before the advent of computer graphics).', 'question': "Topic: American Broadcasting Company\nWhat graphic designer designed the ABC logo in it's most known form?", 'answer': 'Paul Rand'}, {'title': 'American Broadcasting Company', 'context': 'In 1962, graphic designer Paul Rand redesigned the ABC logo into its best-known (and current) form, with the lowercase letters "abc" enclosed in a single black circle. The new logo debuted on-air for ABC\'s promos at the start of the 1963–64 season. The letters are strongly reminiscent of the Bauhaus typeface designed by Herbert Bayer in the 1920s, but also share similarities with several other fonts, such as ITC Avant Garde and Horatio, and most closely resembling Chalet. The logo\'s simplicity made it easier to redesign and duplicate, which conferred a benefit for ABC (mostly before the advent of computer graphics).', 'question': 'Topic: American Broadcasting Company\nWhat typeface are the letters in the iconic ABC logo reminiscent of?', 'answer': 'Bauhaus typeface'}, {'title': 'American Broadcasting Company', 'context': 'In 1962, graphic designer Paul Rand redesigned the ABC logo into its best-known (and current) form, with the lowercase letters "abc" enclosed in a single black circle. The new logo debuted on-air for ABC\'s promos at the start of the 1963–64 season. The letters are strongly reminiscent of the Bauhaus typeface designed by Herbert