am of charged particles. It was believed that this device could be used to deliver energy to a target, for example, for the purposes of transmission of electrical energy, remote lighting, or for medical purposes.', 'Nikola Tesla\nTesla built several versions of this device, and in one of his experiments, he claimed to have successfully demonstrated its capabilities. In 1899, Tesla conducted a public demonstration of his "electric gun" in the Waldorf-Astoria Hotel in New York City. During this demonstration, Tesla fired the beam of charged particles at a piece of metal, and the beam was reported to have melted the metal and caused it to explode.', 'Nikola Tesla\nIt is unclear if Tesla was able to control the range and size of the beam or if the beam could be steered or focused. Tesla believed that the device had great potential for use in various fields and claimed that it could be used for long-distance transmission of energy, communication, and even for warfare.', 'Nikola Tesla\nHowever, there is limited evidence to support Tesla\'s claims, and the device never gained widespread acceptance or use. Tesla continued to work on his "electric gun" in the years that followed, and he patented several variations of the device, but it is not clear how successful or practical they were.', 'Nikola Tesla\nOn 11 July 1934, the New York Herald Tribune published an article on Tesla, in which he recalled an event that would occasionally take place while experimenting with his single-electrode vacuum tubes; a minute particle would break off the cathode, pass out of the tube, and physically strike him. "Tesla said he could feel a sharp stinging pain where it entered his body, and again at the place where it passed out." In comparing these particles with the bits of metal projected by his "electric gun," Tesla said, "The particles in the beam of force ... will travel much faster than such particles ... and they will travel in concentrations."'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Nikola Tesla', 'context': 'On 11 July 1934, the New York Herald Tribune published an article on Tesla, in which he recalled an event that would occasionally take place while experimenting with his single-electrode vacuum tubes; a minute particle would break off the cathode, pass out of the tube, and physically strike him. "Tesla said he could feel a sharp stinging pain where it entered his body, and again at the place where it passed out." In comparing these particles with the bits of metal projected by his "electric gun," Tesla said, "The particles in the beam of force ... will travel much faster than such particles ... and they will travel in concentrations."', 'question': 'Topic: Nikola Tesla\nWhen did the Herald Tribune publish its Tesla article?', 'answer': '1934'}, {'title': 'Nikola Tesla', 'context': 'On 11 July 1934, the New York Herald Tribune published an article on Tesla, in which he recalled an event that would occasionally take place while experimenting with his single-electrode vacuum tubes; a minute particle would break off the cathode, pass out of the tube, and physically strike him. "Tesla said he could feel a sharp stinging pain where it entered his body, and again at the place where it passed out." In comparing these particles with the bits of metal projected by his "electric gun," Tesla said, "The particles in the beam of force ... will travel much faster than such particles ... and they will travel in concentrations."', 'question': 'Topic: Nikola Tesla\nWhat did the particle do to Tesla?', 'answer': 'physically strike him'}, {'title': 'Nikola Tesla', 'context': 'On 11 July 1934, the New York Herald Tribune published an article on Tesla, in which he recalled an event that would occasionally take place while experimenting with his single-electrode vacuum tubes; a minute particle would break off the cathode, pass out of the tube, and physically strike him. "Tesla said he could feel a sharp stinging pain where it entered his body, and again at the place where it passed out." In comparing these particles with the bits of metal projected by his "electric gun," Tesla said, "The particles in the beam of force ... will travel much faster than such particles ... and they will travel in concentrations."', 'question': 'Topic: Nikola Tesla\nHow did Tesla know he was being struck by the particle?', 'answer': 'he could feel a sharp stinging pain where it entered his body'}, {'title': 'Nikola Tesla', 'context': 'On 11 July 1934, the New York Herald Tribune published an article on Tesla, in which he recalled an event that would occasionally take place while experimenting with his single-electrode vacuum tubes; a minute particle would break off the cathode, pass out of the tube, and physically strike him. "Tesla said he could feel a sharp stinging pain where it entered his body, and again at the place where it passed out." In comparing these particles with the bits of metal projected by his "electric gun," Tesla said, "The particles in the beam of force ... will travel much faster than such particles ... and they will travel in concentrations."', 'question': 'Topic: Nikola Tesla\nWhat other projectiles did Tesla compare the electric particles to?', 'answer': 'bits of metal'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
