12:40:19 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Fresno, California\nWhat line homes from the early 20th century in the heart of the Alta Vista tract?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 1:  The section of Huntington Boulevard between First Street on the west to Cedar Avenue on the east.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 2:  Who mapped the Alta Vista Tract in 1910?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 2: William Stranahan for the Pacific Improvement Corporation.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 3:  When was the Alta Vista Tract officially platted?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 3: In 1911.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 4:  What are the boundaries of the Alta Vista Tract?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 4: Balch Avenue on the south, Cedar Avenue on the east, the rear property line of Platt Avenue (east of Sixth Street) and Platt Avenue (west of Sixth Street) on the north, and First Street on the west.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 5:  When was the Alta Vista Tract annexed to the city?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 5: January 1912.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 6:  When was the first election held in which women could vote in Fresno?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 6: At the time of the admission of the Alta Vista Tract to the city.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 7:  What service did Billings & Meyering provide to the tract in 1914?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 7: They completed street development, provided the last of the necessary municipal improvements, including water service, and began marketing the property with fervor.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 8:  In what year was the Alta Vista Tract almost completed?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 8: In 1914.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 9:  What was one reason the Alta Vista Tract was developed rapidly?', "Fresno, California\nAnswer 9: The Fresno Traction Company's right-of-way along Huntington Boulevard provided streetcar connections between downtown and the County Hospital.", "Fresno, California\nLet's read the following passage and rewrite it in a question-answer format.", 'Fresno, California\nPassage:', "Fresno, California\nFive sources of funding remain open. These are the American Field Service (AFS), the Boy Scouts of America (BSA), Girl Scouts of the USA, the Rotary Club International, and All-American Girls Softball Association. Additional funding will be sought from the World Games Federation, the Adopt-An-Athlete program, the U.S. Olympic Committee Athletes' Fund, and the World Champion Athletes program.", 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 1: Who is a source of funding?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 1: American Field Service (AFS)', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 2: Which organization is another source of funding?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 2: Boy Scouts of America (BSA)', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 3: Who is another source of funding?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 3: Girl Scouts of the USA', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 4: Who is another source of funding?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 4: Rotary Club International', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 5: Who is another source of funding?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 5: All-American Girls Softball Association', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 6: From where will additional funding be sought?', "Fresno, California\nAnswer 6: World Games Federation, Adopt-An-Athlete program, U.S. Olympic Committee Athletes' Fund, and the World Champion Athletes program", "Fresno, California\nLet's read the following passage and rewrite it in a question-answer format.", 'Fresno, California\nPassage:', 'Fresno, California\nFred and Ethel had a small child named Larry. Fred and Ethel had no intention of having any other children. They wanted Larry to have a wonderful life. Fred and Ethel were appalled at the thought of an ignorant child living on the streets and sleeping in a doorway.', "Fresno, California\nQuestion 1: What was Fred and Ethel's intention regarding having more children?", 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 1: Fred and Ethel had no intention of having any other children.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 2: What did Fred and Ethel wish for their child Larry?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 2: Fred and Ethel wanted Larry to have a wonderful life.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 3: Did Fred and Ethel want Larry to live on the streets?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 3: No, Fred and Ethel were appalled at the thought of an ignorant child living on the streets.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 4: How did Fred and Ethel feel about a child sleeping in a doorway?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 4: Fred and Ethel were appalled at the thought of a child sleeping in a doorway.', "Fresno, California\nLet's read the following passage and rewrite it in a question-answer format.", 'Fresno, California\nPassage:', 'Fresno, California\nFinance school students examine photos of Michael Eisner, chairman of the Disney board of directors, and Jeffrey Katzenberg, chief creative officer of Walt Disney Studios, inside a classroom in Downtown Disney in 2000.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 1: Who are Michael Eisner and Jeffrey Katzenberg?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 1: Michael Eisner is the chairman of the Disney board of directors, and Jeffrey Katzenberg is the chief creative officer of Walt Disney Studios.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 2: What does the passage indicate about the location of Finance school students?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 2: The passage indicates that the Finance school students were in a classroom in Downtown Disney in 2000.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 3: Are Michael Eisner and Jeffrey Katzenberg related to the topic of the passage?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 3: Yes, Michael Eisner and Jeffrey Katzenberg are related to the topic of the passage as they are both associated with Disney, which is the subject of the passage.', "Fresno, California\nLet's read the following passage and rewrite it in a question-answer format.", 'Fresno, California\nPassage:', 'Fresno, California\nThe 2014 Padre Island Beach Bum Run took place on Saturday, November 15, 2014, along the West Orange Beltway between the oyster shell bike trail at the northern end of the island and the Beach Trail entry at the south end. The route was 4.7 miles (7.6 km) in length. The route was mostly on paved roads, with a small detour on the Shell Beach Rd.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 1: When did the 2014 Padre Island Beach Bum Run take place?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 1: On Saturday, November 15, 2014.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 2: Where did the 2014 Padre Island Beach Bum Run take place?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 2: Along the West Orange Beltway between the oyster shell bike trail at the northern end of the island and the Beach Trail entry at the south end.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 3: What was the length of the 2014 Padre Island Beach Bum Run route?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 3: The route was 4.7 miles (7.6 km) in length.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 4: Was the 2014 Padre Island Beach Bum Run route mostly on paved roads?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 4: Yes, the route was mostly on paved roads.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 5: Was there a small detour on the 2014 Padre Island Beach Bum Run route?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 5: Yes, there was a small detour on the Shell Beach Rd.', "Fresno, California\nHomes from the early 20th century line this boulevard in the heart of the historic Alta Vista Tract. The section of Huntington Boulevard between First Street on the west to Cedar Avenue on the east is the home to many large, stately homes. The original development of this area began circa 1910, on 190 acres of what had been an alfalfa field. The Alta Vista Tract, as the land would become known, was mapped by William Stranahan for the Pacific Improvement Corporation, and was officially platted in 1911. The tract's boundaries were Balch Avenue on the south, Cedar Avenue on the east, the rear property line of Platt Avenue (east of Sixth Street) and Platt Avenue (west of Sixth Street) on the north, and First Street on the west. The subdivision was annexed to the City in January 1912, in an election that was the first in which women voted in the community. At the time of its admission to the City, the Alta Vista Tract was uninhabited but landscaped, although the trees had to be watered by tank wagon. In 1914 developers Billings & Meyering acquired the tract, completed street development, provided the last of the necessary municipal improvements including water service, and began marketing the property with fervor. A mere half decade later the tract had 267 homes. This rapid development was no doubt hastened by the Fresno Traction Company right-of-way along Huntington Boulevard, which provided streetcar connections between downtown and the County Hospital."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Fresno, California', 'context': "Homes from the early 20th century line this boulevard in the heart of the historic Alta Vista Tract. The section of Huntington Boulevard between First Street on the west to Cedar Avenue on the east is the home to many large, stately homes. The original development of this area began circa 1910, on 190 acres of what had been an alfalfa field. The Alta Vista Tract, as the land would become known, was mapped by William Stranahan for the Pacific Improvement Corporation, and was officially platted in 1911. The tract's boundaries were Balch Avenue on the south, Cedar Avenue on the east, the rear property line of Platt Avenue (east of Sixth Street) and Platt Avenue (west of Sixth Street) on the north, and First Street on the west. The subdivision was annexed to the City in January 1912, in an election that was the first in which women voted in the community. At the time of its admission to the City, the Alta Vista Tract was uninhabited but landscaped, although the trees had to be watered by tank wagon. In 1914 developers Billings & Meyering acquired the tract, completed street development, provided the last of the necessary municipal improvements including water service, and began marketing the property with fervor. A mere half decade later the tract had 267 homes. This rapid development was no doubt hastened by the Fresno Traction Company right-of-way along Huntington Boulevard, which provided streetcar connections between downtown and the County Hospital.", 'question': 'Topic: Fresno, California\nWhich boulevard can you find many majestic homes in the area?', 'answer': 'Huntington Boulevard'}, {'title': 'Fresno, California', 'context': "Homes from the early 20th century line this boulevard in the heart of the historic Alta Vista Tract. The section of Huntington Boulevard between First Street on the west to Cedar Avenue on the east is the home to many large, stately homes. The original development of this area began circa 1910, on 190 acres of what had been an alfalfa field. The Alta Vista Tract, as the land would become known, was mapped by William Stranahan for the Pacific Improvement Corporation, and was officially platted in 1911. The tract's boundaries were Balch Avenue on the south, Cedar Avenue on the east, the rear property line of Platt Avenue (east of Sixth Street) and Platt Avenue (west of Sixth Street) on the north, and First Street on the west. The subdivision was annexed to the City in January 1912, in an election that was the first in which women voted in the community. At the time of its admission to the City, the Alta Vista Tract was uninhabited but landscaped, although the trees had to be watered by tank wagon. In 1914 developers Billings & Meyering acquired the tract, completed street development, provided the last of the necessary municipal improvements including water service, and began marketing the property with fervor. A mere half decade later the tract had 267 homes. This rapid development was no doubt hastened by the Fresno Traction Company right-of-way along Huntington Boulevard, which provided streetcar connections between downtown and the County Hospital.", 'question': 'Topic: Fresno, California\nWho mapped the Alta Vista Tract?', 'answer': 'William Stranahan'}, {'title': 'Fresno, California', 'context': "Homes from the early 20th century line this boulevard in the heart of the historic Alta Vista Tract. The section of Huntington Boulevard between First Street on the west to Cedar Avenue on the east is the home to many large, stately homes. The original development of this area began circa 1910, on 190 acres of what had been an alfalfa field. The Alta Vista Tract, as the land would become known, was mapped by William Stranahan for the Pacific Improvement Corporation, and was officially platted in 1911. The tract's boundaries were Balch Avenue on the south, Cedar Avenue on the east, the rear property line of Platt Avenue (east of Sixth Street) and Platt Avenue (west of Sixth Street) on the north, and First Street on the west. The subdivision was annexed to the City in January 1912, in an election that was the first in which women voted in the community. At the time of its admission to the City, the Alta Vista Tract was uninhabited but landscaped, although the trees had to be watered by tank wagon. In 1914 developers Billings & Meyering acquired the tract, completed street development, provided the last of the necessary municipal improvements including water service, and began marketing the property with fervor. A mere half decade later the tract had 267 homes. This rapid development was no doubt hastened by the Fresno Traction Company right-of-way along Huntington Boulevard, which provided streetcar connections between downtown and the County Hospital.", 'question': 'Topic: Fresno, California\nIn what year did developers Billings & Meyering acquire the Alta Vista Tract?', 'answer': '1914'}, {'title': 'Fresno, California', 'context': "Homes from the early 20th century line this boulevard in the heart of the historic Alta Vista Tract. The section of Huntington Boulevard between First Street on the west to Cedar Avenue on the east is the home to many large, stately homes. The original development of this area began circa 1910, on 190 acres of what had been an alfalfa field. The Alta Vista Tract, as the land would become known, was mapped by William Stranahan for the Pacific Improvement Corporation, and was officially platted in 1911. The tract's boundaries were Balch Avenue on the south, Cedar Avenue on the east, the rear property line of Platt Avenue (east of Sixth Street) and Platt Avenue (west of Sixth Street) on the north, and First Street on the west. The subdivision was annexed to the City in January 1912, in an election that was the first in which women voted in the community. At the time of its admission to the City, the Alta Vista Tract was uninhabited but landscaped, although the trees had to be watered by tank wagon. In 1914 developers Billings & Meyering acquired the tract, completed street development, provided the last of the necessary municipal improvements including water service, and began marketing the property with fervor. A mere half decade later the tract had 267 homes. This rapid development was no doubt hastened by the Fresno Traction Company right-of-way along Huntington Boulevard, which provided streetcar connections between downtown and the County Hospital.", 'question': 'Topic: Fresno, California\nHow many homes did the Alta Vista Tract have five years after Billings & Meyering acquired it?', 'answer': '267'}, {'title': 'Fresno, California', 'context': "Homes from the early 20th century line this boulevard in the heart of the historic Alta Vista Tract. The section of Huntington Boulevard between First Street on the west to Cedar Avenue on the east is the home to many large, stately homes. The original development of this area began circa 1910, on 190 acres of what had been an alfalfa field. The Alta Vista Tract, as the land would become known, was mapped by William Stranahan for the Pacific Improvement Corporation, and was officially platted in 1911. The tract's boundaries were Balch Avenue on the south, Cedar Avenue on the east, the rear property line of Platt Avenue (east of Sixth Street) and Platt Avenue (west of Sixth Street) on the north, and First Street on the west. The subdivision was annexed to the City in January 1912, in an election that was the first in which women voted in the community. At the time of its admission to the City, the Alta Vista Tract was uninhabited but landscaped, although the trees had to be watered by tank wagon. In 1914 developers Billings & Meyering acquired the tract, completed street development, provided the last of the necessary municipal improvements including water service, and began marketing the property with fervor. A mere half decade later the tract had 267 homes. This rapid development was no doubt hastened by the Fresno Traction Company right-of-way along Huntington Boulevard, which provided streetcar connections between downtown and the County Hospital.", 'question': 'Topic: Fresno, California\nWhich company provided streetcar connections between downtown and the hospital?', 'answer': 'Fresno Traction Company'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
