12:57:01 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ["Genghis Khan\nWhat were Genghis Khan's accomplishments in unifying smaller tribes?", 'Genghis Khan\nAnswer 1: By 1206, Temüjin had managed to unite or subdue the Merkits, Naimans, Mongols, Keraites, Tatars, Uyghurs, and other disparate smaller tribes under his rule.', 'Genghis Khan\nQuestion 2: How did Genghis Khan achieve this unification?', 'Genghis Khan\nAnswer 2: Genghis Khan achieved this unification by taking the role of "Khan" of the consolidated tribes and taking the title "Genghis Khan" at the Khuruldai, a council of Mongol chiefs.', 'Genghis Khan\nQuestion 3: What was the significance of Genghis Khan taking the title "Khagan"?', 'Genghis Khan\nAnswer 3: Genghis Khan was not awarded the title of "Khagan" until after his death, when his son and successor, Ögedei, took the title for himself and extended it posthumously to his father. This conferred the title of founding the Yuan dynasty on Genghis Khan.', 'Genghis Khan\nQuestion 4: Why was the political and military force under Genghis Khan important?', "Genghis Khan\nAnswer 4: The political and military force under Genghis Khan's leadership established peace between previously warring tribes and created a single force capable of carrying out coordinated military operations.", 'Genghis Khan\nAs a result, by 1206 Temüjin had managed to unite or subdue the Merkits, Naimans, Mongols, Keraites, Tatars, Uyghurs, and other disparate smaller tribes under his rule. It was a monumental feat for the "Mongols" (as they became known collectively). At a Khuruldai, a council of Mongol chiefs, Temüjin was acknowledged as "Khan" of the consolidated tribes and took the new title "Genghis Khan". The title Khagan was not conferred on Genghis until after his death, when his son and successor, Ögedei, took the title for himself and extended it posthumously to his father (as he was also to be posthumously declared the founder of the Yuan dynasty). This unification of all confederations by Genghis Khan established peace between previously warring tribes and a single political and military force under Genghis Khan.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Genghis Khan', 'context': 'As a result, by 1206 Temüjin had managed to unite or subdue the Merkits, Naimans, Mongols, Keraites, Tatars, Uyghurs, and other disparate smaller tribes under his rule. It was a monumental feat for the "Mongols" (as they became known collectively). At a Khuruldai, a council of Mongol chiefs, Temüjin was acknowledged as "Khan" of the consolidated tribes and took the new title "Genghis Khan". The title Khagan was not conferred on Genghis until after his death, when his son and successor, Ögedei, took the title for himself and extended it posthumously to his father (as he was also to be posthumously declared the founder of the Yuan dynasty). This unification of all confederations by Genghis Khan established peace between previously warring tribes and a single political and military force under Genghis Khan.', 'question': 'Topic: Genghis Khan\nBy what year had Temüjin created a significant "Mongol" tribal alliance?', 'answer': '1206'}, {'title': 'Genghis Khan', 'context': 'As a result, by 1206 Temüjin had managed to unite or subdue the Merkits, Naimans, Mongols, Keraites, Tatars, Uyghurs, and other disparate smaller tribes under his rule. It was a monumental feat for the "Mongols" (as they became known collectively). At a Khuruldai, a council of Mongol chiefs, Temüjin was acknowledged as "Khan" of the consolidated tribes and took the new title "Genghis Khan". The title Khagan was not conferred on Genghis until after his death, when his son and successor, Ögedei, took the title for himself and extended it posthumously to his father (as he was also to be posthumously declared the founder of the Yuan dynasty). This unification of all confederations by Genghis Khan established peace between previously warring tribes and a single political and military force under Genghis Khan.', 'question': 'Topic: Genghis Khan\nWhat is the term for a meeting of Mongol chiefs?', 'answer': 'Khuruldai'}, {'title': 'Genghis Khan', 'context': 'As a result, by 1206 Temüjin had managed to unite or subdue the Merkits, Naimans, Mongols, Keraites, Tatars, Uyghurs, and other disparate smaller tribes under his rule. It was a monumental feat for the "Mongols" (as they became known collectively). At a Khuruldai, a council of Mongol chiefs, Temüjin was acknowledged as "Khan" of the consolidated tribes and took the new title "Genghis Khan". The title Khagan was not conferred on Genghis until after his death, when his son and successor, Ögedei, took the title for himself and extended it posthumously to his father (as he was also to be posthumously declared the founder of the Yuan dynasty). This unification of all confederations by Genghis Khan established peace between previously warring tribes and a single political and military force under Genghis Khan.', 'question': 'Topic: Genghis Khan\nWhat title was given to Genghis Khan posthumously?', 'answer': 'Khagan'}, {'title': 'Genghis Khan', 'context': 'As a result, by 1206 Temüjin had managed to unite or subdue the Merkits, Naimans, Mongols, Keraites, Tatars, Uyghurs, and other disparate smaller tribes under his rule. It was a monumental feat for the "Mongols" (as they became known collectively). At a Khuruldai, a council of Mongol chiefs, Temüjin was acknowledged as "Khan" of the consolidated tribes and took the new title "Genghis Khan". The title Khagan was not conferred on Genghis until after his death, when his son and successor, Ögedei, took the title for himself and extended it posthumously to his father (as he was also to be posthumously declared the founder of the Yuan dynasty). This unification of all confederations by Genghis Khan established peace between previously warring tribes and a single political and military force under Genghis Khan.', 'question': 'Topic: Genghis Khan\nWho gave Genghis Khan the title Khadan?', 'answer': 'Ögedei'}, {'title': 'Genghis Khan', 'context': 'As a result, by 1206 Temüjin had managed to unite or subdue the Merkits, Naimans, Mongols, Keraites, Tatars, Uyghurs, and other disparate smaller tribes under his rule. It was a monumental feat for the "Mongols" (as they became known collectively). At a Khuruldai, a council of Mongol chiefs, Temüjin was acknowledged as "Khan" of the consolidated tribes and took the new title "Genghis Khan". The title Khagan was not conferred on Genghis until after his death, when his son and successor, Ögedei, took the title for himself and extended it posthumously to his father (as he was also to be posthumously declared the founder of the Yuan dynasty). This unification of all confederations by Genghis Khan established peace between previously warring tribes and a single political and military force under Genghis Khan.', 'question': 'Topic: Genghis Khan\nWho made Temüjin khan of the Mongols?', 'answer': 'a council of Mongol chiefs'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
