What was the first American V8 to be replaced by the Japanese imports?
13:14:38 [INFO] root - TRAIN[1] 1973 oil crisis
Answer 1: The first American V8 to be replaced by the Japanese imports was the standard V8 engine that was commonly found in large cars during the 1973 oil crisis. These V8 engines were more fuel-hungry compared to the more fuel-efficient four-cylinder engines from Japanese import cars such as Toyota Corona, Toyota Corolla, Datsun B210, Datsun 510, Honda Civic, Mitsubishi Galant (a captive import from Chrysler sold as the Dodge Colt), Subaru DL, and later the Honda Accord. In addition to being more fuel-efficient, these Japanese imports also showcased innovative designs such as unibody construction and front-wheel drive, which became common standards in the automobile industry.
