13:25:11 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Martin Luther\nWhat did Martin Luther reviews and reaffirms in his theses and disputations against the antinomians?', 'Martin Luther\nA: He reviews and reaffirms what has been called the "second use of the law."', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 2:  What is the second use of the law according to Martin Luther?', "Martin Luther\nA: The law as the Holy Spirit's tool to work sorrow over sin in man's heart, thus preparing him for Christ's fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel.", 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 3:  What does Martin Luther say about everything that is used to work sorrow over sin?', "Martin Luther\nA: Everything that is used to work sorrow over sin is called the law, even if it is Christ's life, Christ's death for sin, or God's goodness experienced in creation.", "Martin Luther\nQuestion 4:  What did Luther state about Christ's life, death, and God's goodness being law?", 'Martin Luther\nA: Luther states that these are forms of the law that can work sorrow over sin.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 5:  Why does Luther claim that the law should not be preached to Christians anymore?', 'Martin Luther\nA: Luther believes that Christians are no longer sinners in themselves and that the church consists only of essentially holy people.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 6:  What does Martin Luther believe about the law and sinners?', 'Martin Luther\nA: Luther believes that even if the law is not preached among Christians, the law still exists and sinners still need to experience sorrow over sin.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 7:  What does Luther say about the claim of not preaching the Ten Commandments among Christians?', 'Martin Luther\nA: Luther states that removing the three letters l-a-w from the church does not eliminate the accusing law.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 8:  What does Martin Luther believe about the accusing law?', 'Martin Luther\nA: Luther believes that the accusing law still exists, even if it is not directly preached to Christians.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 9:  What does Martin Luther say about the ten commandments among Christians?', 'Martin Luther\nA: Luther argues that even if the Ten Commandments are not preached among Christians, the law is still present and accusing.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 10:  What does Luther suggest about the church consisting of essentially holy people?', 'Martin Luther\nA: Luther suggests that the church consists only of essentially holy people, and thus does not need the law preached to them.', 'Martin Luther\nIn his theses and disputations against the antinomians, Luther reviews and reaffirms, on the one hand, what has been called the "second use of the law," that is, the law as the Holy Spirit\'s tool to work sorrow over sin in man\'s heart, thus preparing him for Christ\'s fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel. Luther states that everything that is used to work sorrow over sin is called the law, even if it is Christ\'s life, Christ\'s death for sin, or God\'s goodness experienced in creation. Simply refusing to preach the Ten Commandments among Christians – thereby, as it were, removing the three letters l-a-w from the church – does not eliminate the accusing law. Claiming that the law – in any form – should not be preached to Christians anymore would be tantamount to asserting that Christians are no longer sinners in themselves and that the church consists only of essentially holy people.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'In his theses and disputations against the antinomians, Luther reviews and reaffirms, on the one hand, what has been called the "second use of the law," that is, the law as the Holy Spirit\'s tool to work sorrow over sin in man\'s heart, thus preparing him for Christ\'s fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel. Luther states that everything that is used to work sorrow over sin is called the law, even if it is Christ\'s life, Christ\'s death for sin, or God\'s goodness experienced in creation. Simply refusing to preach the Ten Commandments among Christians – thereby, as it were, removing the three letters l-a-w from the church – does not eliminate the accusing law. Claiming that the law – in any form – should not be preached to Christians anymore would be tantamount to asserting that Christians are no longer sinners in themselves and that the church consists only of essentially holy people.', 'question': "Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat did Luther's answer to the antinomians reaffirms?", 'answer': 'second use of the law'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'In his theses and disputations against the antinomians, Luther reviews and reaffirms, on the one hand, what has been called the "second use of the law," that is, the law as the Holy Spirit\'s tool to work sorrow over sin in man\'s heart, thus preparing him for Christ\'s fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel. Luther states that everything that is used to work sorrow over sin is called the law, even if it is Christ\'s life, Christ\'s death for sin, or God\'s goodness experienced in creation. Simply refusing to preach the Ten Commandments among Christians – thereby, as it were, removing the three letters l-a-w from the church – does not eliminate the accusing law. Claiming that the law – in any form – should not be preached to Christians anymore would be tantamount to asserting that Christians are no longer sinners in themselves and that the church consists only of essentially holy people.', 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat is the use of the law by the Holy Spirit?', 'answer': 'work sorrow over sin'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'In his theses and disputations against the antinomians, Luther reviews and reaffirms, on the one hand, what has been called the "second use of the law," that is, the law as the Holy Spirit\'s tool to work sorrow over sin in man\'s heart, thus preparing him for Christ\'s fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel. Luther states that everything that is used to work sorrow over sin is called the law, even if it is Christ\'s life, Christ\'s death for sin, or God\'s goodness experienced in creation. Simply refusing to preach the Ten Commandments among Christians – thereby, as it were, removing the three letters l-a-w from the church – does not eliminate the accusing law. Claiming that the law – in any form – should not be preached to Christians anymore would be tantamount to asserting that Christians are no longer sinners in themselves and that the church consists only of essentially holy people.', 'question': "Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat is Luther's opinion of what the law covers??", 'answer': 'everything'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'In his theses and disputations against the antinomians, Luther reviews and reaffirms, on the one hand, what has been called the "second use of the law," that is, the law as the Holy Spirit\'s tool to work sorrow over sin in man\'s heart, thus preparing him for Christ\'s fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel. Luther states that everything that is used to work sorrow over sin is called the law, even if it is Christ\'s life, Christ\'s death for sin, or God\'s goodness experienced in creation. Simply refusing to preach the Ten Commandments among Christians – thereby, as it were, removing the three letters l-a-w from the church – does not eliminate the accusing law. Claiming that the law – in any form – should not be preached to Christians anymore would be tantamount to asserting that Christians are no longer sinners in themselves and that the church consists only of essentially holy people.', 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat does refusing to preach the Ten Commandments not do?', 'answer': 'eliminate the accusing law'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'In his theses and disputations against the antinomians, Luther reviews and reaffirms, on the one hand, what has been called the "second use of the law," that is, the law as the Holy Spirit\'s tool to work sorrow over sin in man\'s heart, thus preparing him for Christ\'s fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel. Luther states that everything that is used to work sorrow over sin is called the law, even if it is Christ\'s life, Christ\'s death for sin, or God\'s goodness experienced in creation. Simply refusing to preach the Ten Commandments among Christians – thereby, as it were, removing the three letters l-a-w from the church – does not eliminate the accusing law. Claiming that the law – in any form – should not be preached to Christians anymore would be tantamount to asserting that Christians are no longer sinners in themselves and that the church consists only of essentially holy people.', 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat did Luther claim the church would have to comprise if the law were not taught?', 'answer': 'essentially holy people'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
