13:27:47 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Chloroplast\nWhat is Chloroplast', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 1: Chloroplast is a membrane-bound organelle found in plant cells that carries out photosynthesis. It has a unique structure and function that allows it to convert sunlight, carbon dioxide, and water into glucose and oxygen.', 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 2:  What starts the division process in a chloroplast', "Chloroplast\nAnswer 2: The division process in a chloroplast starts with the assembly of proteins FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 into filaments, followed by the help of a protein called ARC6, forming a structure called a Z-ring within the chloroplast's stroma.", 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 3:  What is the Z-ring?', "Chloroplast\nAnswer 3: The Z-ring is a structure formed within the chloroplast's stroma during the division process. It is formed by the assembly of FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 proteins with the help of protein ARC6.", 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 4:  What role does the Min system play in the placement of the Z-ring in a chloroplast', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 4: The Min system manages the placement of the Z-ring in a chloroplast by preventing the proteins MinD and ARC3 from linking up and forming filaments at the poles of the chloroplast. This ensures that the chloroplast is cleaved more or less evenly.', 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 5:  What is the role of the MinD protein in chloroplast division', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 5: The MinD protein prevents FtsZ from linking up and forming filaments, and may also play a role in preventing the Z-ring from forming at the poles of the chloroplast.', 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 6:  What is the role of the ARC3 protein in chloroplast division', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 6: The ARC3 protein may be involved in chloroplast division, but its function is not very well understood.', 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 7:  How does MinE prevent Z-ring formation in a chloroplast', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 7: MinE inhibits the proteins MinD and ARC3, allowing the Z-ring to form near the center of the chloroplast. This ensures an even distribution of the chloroplast during division.', 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 8:  What is the unique function of Chloroplast', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 8: The unique function of chloroplast is to carry out photosynthesis. It converts sunlight, carbon dioxide, and water into glucose and oxygen, providing energy for the plant cell and releasing oxygen as a byproduct.', "Chloroplast\nThe division process starts when the proteins FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 assemble into filaments, and with the help of a protein ARC6, form a structure called a Z-ring within the chloroplast's stroma. The Min system manages the placement of the Z-ring, ensuring that the chloroplast is cleaved more or less evenly. The protein MinD prevents FtsZ from linking up and forming filaments. Another protein ARC3 may also be involved, but it is not very well understood. These proteins are active at the poles of the chloroplast, preventing Z-ring formation there, but near the center of the chloroplast, MinE inhibits them, allowing the Z-ring to form."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': "The division process starts when the proteins FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 assemble into filaments, and with the help of a protein ARC6, form a structure called a Z-ring within the chloroplast's stroma. The Min system manages the placement of the Z-ring, ensuring that the chloroplast is cleaved more or less evenly. The protein MinD prevents FtsZ from linking up and forming filaments. Another protein ARC3 may also be involved, but it is not very well understood. These proteins are active at the poles of the chloroplast, preventing Z-ring formation there, but near the center of the chloroplast, MinE inhibits them, allowing the Z-ring to form.", 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhat do FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 combine into?', 'answer': 'filaments'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': "The division process starts when the proteins FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 assemble into filaments, and with the help of a protein ARC6, form a structure called a Z-ring within the chloroplast's stroma. The Min system manages the placement of the Z-ring, ensuring that the chloroplast is cleaved more or less evenly. The protein MinD prevents FtsZ from linking up and forming filaments. Another protein ARC3 may also be involved, but it is not very well understood. These proteins are active at the poles of the chloroplast, preventing Z-ring formation there, but near the center of the chloroplast, MinE inhibits them, allowing the Z-ring to form.", 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhat are FtsZ1 and FtsZ2?', 'answer': 'proteins'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': "The division process starts when the proteins FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 assemble into filaments, and with the help of a protein ARC6, form a structure called a Z-ring within the chloroplast's stroma. The Min system manages the placement of the Z-ring, ensuring that the chloroplast is cleaved more or less evenly. The protein MinD prevents FtsZ from linking up and forming filaments. Another protein ARC3 may also be involved, but it is not very well understood. These proteins are active at the poles of the chloroplast, preventing Z-ring formation there, but near the center of the chloroplast, MinE inhibits them, allowing the Z-ring to form.", 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhat do FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 plus ARC6 form?', 'answer': 'a structure called a Z-ring'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': "The division process starts when the proteins FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 assemble into filaments, and with the help of a protein ARC6, form a structure called a Z-ring within the chloroplast's stroma. The Min system manages the placement of the Z-ring, ensuring that the chloroplast is cleaved more or less evenly. The protein MinD prevents FtsZ from linking up and forming filaments. Another protein ARC3 may also be involved, but it is not very well understood. These proteins are active at the poles of the chloroplast, preventing Z-ring formation there, but near the center of the chloroplast, MinE inhibits them, allowing the Z-ring to form.", 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhere is the Z-ring?', 'answer': "within the chloroplast's stroma"}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': "The division process starts when the proteins FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 assemble into filaments, and with the help of a protein ARC6, form a structure called a Z-ring within the chloroplast's stroma. The Min system manages the placement of the Z-ring, ensuring that the chloroplast is cleaved more or less evenly. The protein MinD prevents FtsZ from linking up and forming filaments. Another protein ARC3 may also be involved, but it is not very well understood. These proteins are active at the poles of the chloroplast, preventing Z-ring formation there, but near the center of the chloroplast, MinE inhibits them, allowing the Z-ring to form.", 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhat gets the Z-ring in the right place?', 'answer': 'The Min system'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
