l?', "United Methodist Church\nAnswer 8: The passage doesn't mention the exact number of members in the World Methodist Council, only that it is composed of various churches in the tradition of John Wesley.", 'United Methodist Church\nQuestion 9: What is the goal of the World Methodist Council?', 'United Methodist Church\nAnswer 9: The goal of the World Methodist Council is to promote the Gospel throughout the world.', 'United Methodist Church\nQuestion 10: Why is the World Methodist Council considered an interdenominational group?', 'United Methodist Church\nAnswer 10: The World Methodist Council is considered an interdenominational group because it is composed of various churches in the tradition of John Wesley.', "United Methodist Church\nQuestion 11: What is the United Methodist Church's membership status in the Wesleyan Holiness Consortium?", 'United Methodist Church\nAnswer 11: The UMC is a member of the Wesleyan Holiness Consortium.', 'United Methodist Church\nQuestion 12: What does the Wesleyan Holiness Consortium aim to achieve?', "United Methodist Church\nAnswer 12: The Wesleyan Holiness Consortium aims to reconceive and promote Biblical holiness in today's Church.", 'United Methodist Church\nQuestion 13: How do the World Methodist Council and the Wesleyan Holiness Consortium interact?', 'United Methodist Church\nAnswer 13: There is no specific interaction mentioned between the World Methodist Council and the Wesleyan Holiness Consortium in the passage.', 'United Methodist Church\nQuestion 14: Who ultimately approves new decisions or declarations made by the World Methodist Council?', "United Methodist Church\nAnswer 14: The passage doesn't explicitly mention who ultimately approves new decisions or declarations made by the World Methodist Council, but the approval of the delegates on July 18, 2006, suggests that they are part of the decision-making process.", 'United Methodist Church\nQuestion 15: What is the significance of the "Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification" for the UMC?', 'United Methodist Church\nAnswer 15: The "Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification" was approved in 1999 by the Vatican and the Lutheran World Federation. While the passage doesn\'t specify the UMC\'s significance to this declaration, it does imply that the UMC might have been influenced or informed by it.', 'United Methodist Church\nQuestion 16: How is the World Methodist Council composed?', 'United Methodist Church\nAnswer 16: The World Methodist Council is composed of various churches in the tradition of John Wesley.', 'United Methodist Church\nThe UMC is also a member of the Wesleyan Holiness Consortium, which seeks to reconceive and promote Biblical holiness in today\'s Church. It is also active in the World Methodist Council, an interdenominational group composed of various churches in the tradition of John Wesley to promote the Gospel throughout the world. On July 18, 2006, delegates to the World Methodist Council voted unanimously to adopt the "Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification", which was approved in 1999 by the Vatican and the Lutheran World Federation.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'United Methodist Church', 'context': 'The UMC is also a member of the Wesleyan Holiness Consortium, which seeks to reconceive and promote Biblical holiness in today\'s Church. It is also active in the World Methodist Council, an interdenominational group composed of various churches in the tradition of John Wesley to promote the Gospel throughout the world. On July 18, 2006, delegates to the World Methodist Council voted unanimously to adopt the "Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification", which was approved in 1999 by the Vatican and the Lutheran World Federation.', 'question': "Topic: United Methodist Church\nWhich group seeks to reconceive and promote Biblical holiness in today's church?", 'answer': 'Wesleyan Holiness Consortium'}, {'title': 'United Methodist Church', 'context': 'The UMC is also a member of the Wesleyan Holiness Consortium, which seeks to reconceive and promote Biblical holiness in today\'s Church. It is also active in the World Methodist Council, an interdenominational group composed of various churches in the tradition of John Wesley to promote the Gospel throughout the world. On July 18, 2006, delegates to the World Methodist Council voted unanimously to adopt the "Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification", which was approved in 1999 by the Vatican and the Lutheran World Federation.', 'question': 'Topic: United Methodist Church\nWhat is an interdenominational group composed of various churches to promote the Gospel throughout the world?', 'answer': 'World Methodist Council'}, {'title': 'United Methodist Church', 'context': 'The UMC is also a member of the Wesleyan Holiness Consortium, which seeks to reconceive and promote Biblical holiness in today\'s Church. It is also active in the World Methodist Council, an interdenominational group composed of various churches in the tradition of John Wesley to promote the Gospel throughout the world. On July 18, 2006, delegates to the World Methodist Council voted unanimously to adopt the "Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification", which was approved in 1999 by the Vatican and the Lutheran World Federation.', 'question': 'Topic: United Methodist Church\nWhen did delegates to the World Methodist Council vote to adopt the joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification?"', 'answer': 'July 18, 2006'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
