13:40:54 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['French and Indian War\nHow did the French use trading connections to recruit fighters in the French and Indian War?', 'French and Indian War\nQuestion 2:  Who dominated the Southeast interior in the French and Indian War?', 'French and Indian War\nQuestion 3:  Who supported the British in the French and Indian War?', 'French and Indian War\nQuestion 4:  Who supported the French in the French and Indian War?', 'French and Indian War\nQuestion 5:  What was the Treaty of Easton?', 'French and Indian War\nQuestion 6:  What was the Anglo-Cherokee War?', 'French and Indian War\nQuestion 7:  How were the Creek and Cherokee tribes affected by diplomatic efforts of both the French and British?', 'French and Indian War\nQuestion 8:  What was the purpose of most tribes in their decentralized way of warfare?', 'French and Indian War\nFurther south the Southeast interior was dominated by Siouan-speaking Catawba, Muskogee-speaking Creek and Choctaw, and the Iroquoian-speaking Cherokee tribes. When war broke out, the French used their trading connections to recruit fighters from tribes in western portions of the Great Lakes region (an area not directly subject to the conflict between the French and British), including the Huron, Mississauga, Ojibwa, Winnebago, and Potawatomi. The British were supported in the war by the Iroquois Six Nations, and also by the Cherokee – until differences sparked the Anglo-Cherokee War in 1758. In 1758 the Pennsylvania government successfully negotiated the Treaty of Easton, in which a number of tribes in the Ohio Country promised neutrality in exchange for land concessions and other considerations. Most of the other northern tribes sided with the French, their primary trading partner and supplier of arms. The Creek and Cherokee were subject to diplomatic efforts by both the French and British to gain either their support or neutrality in the conflict. It was not uncommon for small bands to participate on the "other side" of the conflict from formally negotiated agreements, as most tribes were decentralized and bands made their own decisions about warfare.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'French and Indian War', 'context': 'Further south the Southeast interior was dominated by Siouan-speaking Catawba, Muskogee-speaking Creek and Choctaw, and the Iroquoian-speaking Cherokee tribes. When war broke out, the French used their trading connections to recruit fighters from tribes in western portions of the Great Lakes region (an area not directly subject to the conflict between the French and British), including the Huron, Mississauga, Ojibwa, Winnebago, and Potawatomi. The British were supported in the war by the Iroquois Six Nations, and also by the Cherokee – until differences sparked the Anglo-Cherokee War in 1758. In 1758 the Pennsylvania government successfully negotiated the Treaty of Easton, in which a number of tribes in the Ohio Country promised neutrality in exchange for land concessions and other considerations. Most of the other northern tribes sided with the French, their primary trading partner and supplier of arms. The Creek and Cherokee were subject to diplomatic efforts by both the French and British to gain either their support or neutrality in the conflict. It was not uncommon for small bands to participate on the "other side" of the conflict from formally negotiated agreements, as most tribes were decentralized and bands made their own decisions about warfare.', 'question': 'Topic: French and Indian War\nWhat are the Siouan-speaking tribes?', 'answer': 'Catawba, Muskogee-speaking Creek and Choctaw'}, {'title': 'French and Indian War', 'context': 'Further south the Southeast interior was dominated by Siouan-speaking Catawba, Muskogee-speaking Creek and Choctaw, and the Iroquoian-speaking Cherokee tribes. When war broke out, the French used their trading connections to recruit fighters from tribes in western portions of the Great Lakes r