13:52:57 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Steam engine\nIn which year was the first commercially successful true engine invented by Thomas Newcomen?', 'Steam engine\nAnswer 1: Around 1712.', 'Steam engine\nQuestion 2: What was the first commercially successful true engine named?', 'Steam engine\nAnswer 2: Atmospheric engine.', "Steam engine\nQuestion 3: How did Newcomen's engine differ from Savery's steam pump?", "Steam engine\nAnswer 3: Newcomen's engine used a piston, while Savery's steam pump did not.", "Steam engine\nQuestion 4: What was the primary use of Newcomen's atmospheric engine?", 'Steam engine\nAnswer 4: The engine was primarily used for pumping water.', "Steam engine\nQuestion 5: What was the primary method of generating power in Newcomen's engine?", 'Steam engine\nAnswer 5: The engine generated power by creating a partial vacuum by condensing steam under a piston within a cylinder.', "Steam engine\nQuestion 6: What were the two main applications of Newcomen's engine?", 'Steam engine\nAnswer 6: The engine was used for draining mine workings at depths hitherto impossible and for providing a reusable water supply for driving waterwheels at factories sited away from a suitable "head".', "Steam engine\nQuestion 7: What was the main function of the water pumped back into a storage reservoir in Newcomen's engine?", 'Steam engine\nAnswer 7: The main function of the water pumped back was to provide a reusable water supply for driving waterwheels at factories.', 'Steam engine\nThe first commercially successful true engine, in that it could generate power and transmit it to a machine, was the atmospheric engine, invented by Thomas Newcomen around 1712. It was an improvement over Savery\'s steam pump, using a piston as proposed by Papin. Newcomen\'s engine was relatively inefficient, and in most cases was used for pumping water. It worked by creating a partial vacuum by condensing steam under a piston within a cylinder. It was employed for draining mine workings at depths hitherto impossible, and also for providing a reusable water supply for driving waterwheels at factories sited away from a suitable "head". Water that had passed over the wheel was pumped back up into a storage reservoir above the wheel.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Steam engine', 'context': 'The first commercially successful true engine, in that it could generate power and transmit it to a machine, was the atmospheric engine, invented by Thomas Newcomen around 1712. It was an improvement over Savery\'s steam pump, using a piston as proposed by Papin. Newcomen\'s engine was relatively inefficient, and in most cases was used for pumping water. It worked by creating a partial vacuum by condensing steam under a piston within a cylinder. It was employed for draining mine workings at depths hitherto impossible, and also for providing a reusable water supply for driving waterwheels at factories sited away from a suitable "head". Water that had passed over the wheel was pumped back up into a storage reservoir above the wheel.', 'question': 'Topic: Steam engine\nWhat was the first true engine that was commercially successful?', 'answer': 'atmospheric engine'}, {'title': 'Steam engine', 'context': 'The first commercially successful true engine, in that it could generate power and transmit it to a machine, was the atmospheric engine, invented by Thomas Newcomen around 1712. It was an improvement over Savery\'s steam pump, using a piston as proposed by Papin. Newcomen\'s engine was relatively inefficient, and in most cases was used for pumping water. It worked by creating a partial vacuum by condensing steam under a piston within a cylinder. It was employed for draining mine workings at depths hitherto impossible, and also for providing a reusable water supply for driving waterwheels at factories sited away from a suitable "head". Water that had passed over the wheel was pumped back up into a storage reservoir above the wheel.', 'question': '