13:53:37 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['University of Chicago\nIs the University of Chicago known for any economic, social, or scientific developments?', 'University of Chicago\nAnswer 1: Yes, the University of Chicago is known for important experiments and academic movements in economics, sociology, physics, and chemistry.', 'University of Chicago\nQuestion 2:  What is the Chicago school of economics?', 'University of Chicago\nAnswer 2: The Chicago school of economics is a school of economic thought supported by Milton Friedman and other economists, and the university is the namesake of this school.', 'University of Chicago\nQuestion 3:  Who developed radiocarbon dating?', 'University of Chicago\nAnswer 3: Willard F. Libby developed radiocarbon dating in 1947.', 'University of Chicago\nQuestion 4:  What significant scientific discovery was made at the University of Chicago in 1953?', 'University of Chicago\nAnswer 4: REM sleep was discovered at the university in 1953 by Nathaniel Kleitman and Eugene Aserinsky.', 'University of Chicago\nQuestion 5:  What is the importance of the Miller–Urey experiment at the University of Chicago?', 'University of Chicago\nAnswer 5: The Miller–Urey experiment, which tested how life originated on early Earth, was conducted at the university.', "University of Chicago\nThe University of Chicago has been the site of some important experiments and academic movements. In economics, the university has played an important role in shaping ideas about the free market and is the namesake of the Chicago school of economics, the school of economic thought supported by Milton Friedman and other economists. The university's sociology department was the first independent sociology department in the United States and gave birth to the Chicago school of sociology. In physics, the university was the site of the Chicago Pile-1 (the first self-sustained man-made nuclear reaction, part of the Manhattan Project), of Robert Millikan's oil-drop experiment that calculated the charge of the electron, and of the development of radiocarbon dating by Willard F. Libby in 1947. The chemical experiment that tested how life originated on early Earth, the Miller–Urey experiment, was conducted at the university. REM sleep was discovered at the university in 1953 by Nathaniel Kleitman and Eugene Aserinsky."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'University of Chicago', 'context': "The University of Chicago has been the site of some important experiments and academic movements. In economics, the university has played an important role in shaping ideas about the free market and is the namesake of the Chicago school of economics, the school of economic thought supported by Milton Friedman and other economists. The university's sociology department was the first independent sociology department in the United States and gave birth to the Chicago school of sociology. In physics, the university was the site of the Chicago Pile-1 (the first self-sustained man-made nuclear reaction, part of the Manhattan Project), of Robert Millikan's oil-drop experiment that calculated the charge of the electron, and of the development of radiocarbon dating by Willard F. Libby in 1947. The chemical experiment that tested how life originated on early Earth, the Miller–Urey experiment, was conducted at the university. REM sleep was discovered at the university in 1953 by Nathaniel Kleitman and Eugene Aserinsky.", 'question': 'Topic: University of Chicago\nWhat role in economics did the university play a major part in?', 'answer': 'shaping ideas about the free market'}, {'title': 'University of Chicago', 'context': "The University of Chicago has been the site of some important experiments and academic movements. In economics, the university has played an important role in shaping ideas about the free market and is the namesake of the Chicago school of economics, the school of economic thought supported by Milton Friedman and other economists. The university's sociology department was the first independent sociology department in the United States and gave birth to the Chicago school of sociology. In physics, the university was the site of the Chicago Pile-1 (the first self-sustained man-made nuclear reaction, part of the Manhattan Project), of Robert Millikan's oil-drop experiment that calculated the charge of the electron, and of the development of radiocarbon dating by Willard F. Libby in 1947. The chemical experiment that tested how life originated on early Earth, the Miller–Urey experiment, was conducted at the university. REM sleep was discovered at the university in 1953 by Nathaniel Kleitman and Eugene Aserinsky.", 'question': 'Topic: University of Chicago\nWhat was the name of the first self-sustained man-made nuclear reaction?', 'answer': 'Chicago Pile-1'}, {'title': 'University of Chicago', 'context': "The University of Chicago has been the site of some important experiments and academic movements. In economics, the university has played an important role in shaping ideas about the free market and is the namesake of the Chicago school of economics, the school of economic thought supported by Milton Friedman and other economists. The university's sociology department was the first independent sociology department in the United States and gave birth to the Chicago school of sociology. In physics, the university was the site of the Chicago Pile-1 (the first self-sustained man-made nuclear reaction, part of the Manhattan Project), of Robert Millikan's oil-drop experiment that calculated the charge of the electron, and of the development of radiocarbon dating by Willard F. Libby in 1947. The chemical experiment that tested how life originated on early Earth, the Miller–Urey experiment, was conducted at the university. REM sleep was discovered at the university in 1953 by Nathaniel Kleitman and Eugene Aserinsky.", 'question': 'Topic: University of Chicago\nWhat was the name of the experiment that tested how life originated?', 'answer': 'Miller–Urey experiment'}, {'title': 'University of Chicago', 'context': "The University of Chicago has been the site of some important experiments and academic movements. In economics, the university has played an important role in shaping ideas about the free market and is the namesake of the Chicago school of economics, the school of economic thought supported by Milton Friedman and other economists. The university's sociology department was the first independent sociology department in the United States and gave birth to the Chicago school of sociology. In physics, the university was the site of the Chicago Pile-1 (the first self-sustained man-made nuclear reaction, part of the Manhattan Project), of Robert Millikan's oil-drop experiment that calculated the charge of the electron, and of the development of radiocarbon dating by Willard F. Libby in 1947. The chemical experiment that tested how life originated on early Earth, the Miller–Urey experiment, was conducted at the university. REM sleep was discovered at the university in 1953 by Nathaniel Kleitman and Eugene Aserinsky.", 'question': 'Topic: University of Chicago\nWhen was REM sleep discovered?', 'answer': '1953'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
