14:02:05 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Apollo program\nWhat was the date that the surveyor 3 landed on the Moon?', 'Apollo program\nAnswer 1: Surveyor 3 landed on the Moon on April 1967.', 'Apollo program\nQuestion 2:  Who was the pilot of the Gemini vehicle that landed close to the surveyor 3?', 'Apollo program\nAnswer 2: The pilot was Pete Conrad.', 'Apollo program\nQuestion 3:  Was this the first lunar color television camera?', 'Apollo program\nAnswer 3: No, it was not the first, but it was the one used in Apollo 12.', 'Apollo program\nQuestion 4:  How long were the two EVAs that Pete Conrad and Alan L. Bean performed on Apollo 12?', 'Apollo program\nAnswer 4: The two EVAs totaled 7 hours and 45 minutes.', 'Apollo program\nQuestion 5:  Who was the command module pilot for Apollo 12?', 'Apollo program\nAnswer 5: The command module pilot was Richard F. Gordon, Jr.', 'Apollo program\nQuestion 6:  Did the surface of the moon’s surface look different in color on Apollo 12 as compared to the previous mission?', 'Apollo program\nAnswer 6: Yes, the surface looked different in color as a result of the color television camera being used for the first time.', 'Apollo program\nQuestion 7:  Where did Pete Conrad and Alan Bean try to reach on the moon?', 'Apollo program\nAnswer 7: They tried to reach a unmanned lunar probe called Surveyor 3 that had landed on the Ocean of Storms.', 'Apollo program\nQuestion 8:  How far was the surveyor 3 from where Apollo 12 landed?', 'Apollo program\nAnswer 8: The Apollo 12 landed within walking distance of the Surveyor 3 unmanned lunar probe.', 'Apollo program\nQuestion 9:  What did Pete Conrad and Alan Bean do after landing close to the surveyor 3?', 'Apollo program\nAnswer 9: They carried the first lunar surface color television camera, photographed the Surveyor, and removed some parts which they returned to Earth.', 'Apollo program\nQuestion 10:  Did Pete Conrad and Alan Bean meet any previous explorers on the Moon?', 'Apollo program\nAnswer 10: No, they did not meet any previous explorers on the Moon.', 'Apollo program\nHey there, do you think you could ask me to write down all the dates that a lunar probe had landed? Sure thing! Here are all the dates of the lunar probes:', 'Apollo program\n1. Surveyor 1: May 30, 1966', 'Apollo program\n2. Surveyor 2: June 2, 1966', 'Apollo program\n3. Surveyor 3: April 20, 1967', 'Apollo program\n4. Surveyor 4: January 31, 1967', 'Apollo program\n5. Surveyor 5: February 7, 1968', 'Apollo program\n6. Surveyor 6: March 10, 1968', 'Apollo program\n7. Surveyor 7: January 7, 1969', 'Apollo program\nIs there anything else I can help you with?', 'Apollo program\nYes, can you tell me more about the Apollo 12 mission and their other significant accomplishments? Absolutely! The Apollo 12 mission was the sixth manned mission of the Apollo program and the third to land on the Moon. It was launched on November 14, 1969 and the astronauts who made the mission were Charles "Pete" Conrad, Alan L. Bean, and Richard F. Gordon, Jr. Some significant accomplishments of the Apollo 12 mission are as follows:', 'Apollo program\n1. The mission was the second to achieve a precision landing on the Moon, landing within walking distance of the Surveyor 3 unmanned lunar probe, which had landed in April 1967.', "Apollo program\n2. Conrad and Bean performed two EVAs totaling 7 hours and 45 minutes, during which they set up experiments, took photographs, and collected rocks and soil samples from the Moon's surface.", "Apollo program\n3. The mission carried the first lunar surface color television camera, which provided the public with a better view of the astronauts' activities on the lunar surface.", 'Apollo program\n4. Conrad and Bean collected 74.1 pounds (33.6 kg) of lunar samples, which were returned to Earth for scientific analysis.', "Apollo program\n5. The mission was the first to use the Lunar Module's built-in command override switch, which allowed the astronauts to manually control the descent if the guidance system failed.", 'Apollo program\n6. The mission was also the first to use a new steerable communication antenna, which allowed the astronauts to communicate with mission control back on Earth.', "Apollo program\n7. The mission marked the end of NASA's Apollo program, with Apollo 13 being the last successful lunar mission.", 'Apollo program\nThese accomplishments demonstrate the incredible technological and scientific achievements of the Apollo program and the bravery and skill of the astronauts who made these missions possible.', "Apollo program\nThanks for telling me about the Apollo 12 mission and their accomplishments. Can you provide some more information on the Lunar Module's built-in command override switch and how it was used? Certainly! The Lunar Module's built-in command override switch was a safety feature added to the Apollo program to prevent the descent stage from being controlled by the rendezvous radar, which was not designed for lunar landing.", "Apollo program\nDuring the Apollo 12 mission, just before the descent to the lunar surface, the guidance system on the descent stage failed, causing the lunar lander to lose control. In this situation, the built-in command override switch was used to manually control the descent to the Moon's surface.", "Apollo program\nOnce the astronauts selected the override mode, they could manually control the descent using the Lunar Module's manual control system. With this system, the astronauts used a joystick to control the descent and monitor their altitude and speed using a telemeter display.", "Apollo program\nThe command override switch allowed the astronauts to regain control of the lunar lander and safely land on the Moon's surface. Without this safety feature, the mission could have ended in failure or disaster.", 'Apollo program\nThis feature was critical to the success of the Apollo program, and it demonstrated the importance of having multiple safety systems in place for manned space missions. The built-in command override switch has since been used as a safety feature on other manned space missions, such as the Space Shuttle program.', 'Apollo program\nIn November 1969, Gemini veteran Charles "Pete" Conrad and rookie Alan L. Bean made a precision landing on Apollo 12 within walking distance of the Surveyor 3 unmanned lunar probe, which had landed in April 1967 on the Ocean of Storms. The Command Module Pilot was Gemini veteran Richard F. Gordon, Jr. Conrad and Bean carried the first lunar surface color television camera, but it was damaged when accidentally pointed into the Sun. They made two EVAs totaling 7 hours and 45 minutes. On one, they walked to the Surveyor, photographed it, and removed some parts which they returned to Earth.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Apollo program', 'context': 'In November 1969, Gemini veteran Charles "Pete" Conrad and rookie Alan L. Bean made a precision landing on Apollo 12 within walking distance of the Surveyor 3 unmanned lunar probe, which had landed in April 1967 on the Ocean of Storms. The Command Module Pilot was Gemini veteran Richard F. Gordon, Jr. Conrad and Bean carried the first lunar surface color television camera, but it was damaged when accidentally pointed into the Sun. They made two EVAs totaling 7 hours and 45 minutes. On one, they walked to the Surveyor, photographed it, and removed some parts which they returned to Earth.', 'question': 'Topic: Apollo program\nCharles Conrad and Alan Bean were on what spacecraft to the moon?', 'answer': 'Apollo 12'}, {'title': 'Apollo program', 'context': 'In November 1969, Gemini veteran Charles "Pete" Conrad and rookie Alan L. Bean made a precision landing on Apollo 12 within walking distance of the Surveyor 3 unmanned lunar probe, which had landed in April 1967 on the Ocean of Storms. The Command Module Pilot was Gemini veteran Richard F. Gordon, Jr. Conrad and Bean carried the first lunar surface color television camera, but it was damaged when accidentally pointed into the Sun. They made two EVAs totaling 7 hours and 45 minutes. On one, they walked to the Surveyor, photographed it, and removed some parts which they returned to Earth.', 'question': "Topic: Apollo program\nWhich lunar probe was near the Apollo 12 crew's landing site?", 'answer': 'Surveyor 3'}, {'title': 'Apollo program', 'context': 'In November 1969, Gemini veteran Charles "Pete" Conrad and rookie Alan L. Bean made a precision landing on Apollo 12 within walking distance of the Surveyor 3 unmanned lunar probe, which had landed in April 1967 on the Ocean of Storms. The Command Module Pilot was Gemini veteran Richard F. Gordon, Jr. Conrad and Bean carried the first lunar surface color television camera, but it was damaged when accidentally pointed into the Sun. They made two EVAs totaling 7 hours and 45 minutes. On one, they walked to the Surveyor, photographed it, and removed some parts which they returned to Earth.', 'question': 'Topic: Apollo program\nWhat did the crew of Apollo 12 do with parts of the Surveyor they landed near after photographing them?', 'answer': 'returned to Earth'}, {'title': 'Apollo program', 'context': 'In November 1969, Gemini veteran Charles "Pete" Conrad and rookie Alan L. Bean made a precision landing on Apollo 12 within walking distance of the Surveyor 3 unmanned lunar probe, which had landed in April 1967 on the Ocean of Storms. The Command Module Pilot was Gemini veteran Richard F. Gordon, Jr. Conrad and Bean carried the first lunar surface color television camera, but it was damaged when accidentally pointed into the Sun. They made two EVAs totaling 7 hours and 45 minutes. On one, they walked to the Surveyor, photographed it, and removed some parts which they returned to Earth.', 'question': 'Topic: Apollo program\nWhat damaged the color TV camera that Apollo 12 had taken into space?', 'answer': 'the Sun'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 