14:06:55 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Doctor Who\nWhat song does the whole orchestra play together in The Race in Space?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 1: The whole orchestra plays the theme tune to Doctor Who during "The Race in Space", and it also appears in some earlier scenes of the show.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 2:  What instruments do the Daleks and Cybermen use to signal during the cliffhanger at the end of the doctor who episode of deludes of the daleks?', "Doctor Who\nAnswer 2: The Daleks use their ship's main cannon to fire a space torpedo, which explodes upon impact, while the Cybermen raise their Tor or Grid-Master to emit a high-pitched whine, signalling to the other Cybermen.", 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 3:  What do the Daleks use to taunt the doctor who episode called "asylum of sound"?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 3: The Daleks, disguised as Exterminator scanners, use "Boom Tube" devices to taunt the doctor with magnetic waves, disrupt his timestream, and keep the story\'s timeline from ever ending.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 4:  What are the names of the main characters in the 3rd doctor who novel that recounts the tragedy of Lovers Rock?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 4: The main characters in the 3rd Doctor Who novel that recounts the tragedy of Lovers Rock are the Doctor, his companions, and the Daleks.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 5:  What did the newest Doctor Who race against in the Golden Dalek post production edition of a?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 5: The newest Doctor Who race in the Golden Dalek post production edition is called "Asylum of Sound," and the Doctor races against an assortment of different monsters and aliens in a high-speed chase through space and time.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 6:  Who wins the race in Asylum of Sound?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 6: In "Asylum of Sound," the Doctor and his companions ultimately escape from the Daleks and their robotic allies by passing through a vortex of space and time, thus winning the race.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 7:  What was a central theme in the story called, the wedding of the daleks?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 7: A central theme in the story called "The Wedding of the Daleks" was the love story between two Daleks, Taron and Raag, which ended in tragedy, causing a rift within the Dalek race that ultimately led to their downfall.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 8:  What type of device was used to prevent the dark sun from destroying the planet in the 2nd doctor who episode?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 8: In the 2nd Doctor Who episode "The Power of the Daleks," a device called a "timestream projector" was used to prevent the dark sun from destroying the planet.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 9:  Who was the creator of the all powerful final empire?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 9: The creator of the all-powerful Final Empire in the 1st Doctor Who novel "The Daleks in Manhattan" is an unknown entity known only as "The Voice of God."', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 10:  What type of device are love stories called in Doctor Who?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 10: Love stories in Doctor Who are called "osmium stroboscopes," a type of lamp that hovers and illuminates the room, revealing the secrets of the relationship between the Doctor and his companions.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 11:  What did the alien in the episode called Asylum of Sound that battled the doctor and his companions called, call the doctor?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 11: In the episode "Asylum of Sound," the alien called the Doctor "The Nihilist," as he believes the Doctor has no morals, ethics, or compassion, and is only interested in himself.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 12:  Who does the alien love that he speaks of in the episode called asylum of sound?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 12: The alien in "Asylum of Sound" is not in love with anyone in the traditional sense. His words are an attempt to evoke emotion and guilt in the Doctor, who he sees as a lonely, twisted individual.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 13:  How is the story of the alien that battled the doctor in asylum of sound told?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 13: The story of the alien that battled the Doctor in "Asylum of Sound" is told through a series of flashbacks, where the alien, who is believed to be the Doctor\'s unknown child, shares his experiences of growing up with the Daleks.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 14:  What does the alien, called Christopher, see himself as?', "Doctor Who\nAnswer 14: The alien, who is later revealed to be the Doctor's son, sees himself as a counter-revolutionary for the Daleks, fighting against their oppressive regime, but he ultimately ends up being more of a symbol of hope and compassion for the Doctor and his companions.", 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 15:  What type of troop does the alien that battled the doctor in asylum of sound resemble?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 15: The alien that battled the Doctor in "Asylum of Sound" resembles a human in form, but he is actually a very robotic creature, with a mind of his own, and a willingness to defy the Daleks and fight for what he believes is right.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 16:  What type of ultimate energy makes the universe?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 16: In Doctor Who, the ultimate energy that makes the universe is called "The Great Tree," which is a living entity that maintains the balance of the universe by regulating the life force of planets and their inhabitants.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 17:  What is the symbol of the evil forces in the universe?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 17: In Doctor Who, the symbol of evil forces in the universe is the Daleks, who are a race of robotic beings that were once humans, but were transformed into mindless killing machines by the Dalek Supreme.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 18:  What is the name of the lowest level of corruption in the universe?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 18: In Doctor Who, the lowest level of corruption in the universe is represented by the Cybermen, who are androids that were once human beings, but were replaced by a cybernetic body designed to serve their masters, the Cybermen.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 19:  What type of energy does the doctor use to defeat the alien called Christopher in asylum of sound?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 19: The Doctor uses a combination of his psychic abilities, his knowledge of technology and the universe, and his compassion for the alien Christopher, in order to defeat him and bring an end to his fight against the Daleks.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 20:  What is the name of the original Dalek, that the alien Christopher talked about in asylum of sound?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 20: The name of the original Dalek in "Asylum of Sound" is not explicitly revealed, but he is later identified as the Grand Dalek, the leader of the Dalek Empire, who is responsible for the creation of the Daleks.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 21:  What was the back-story behind the tragedy that led to the creation of the daleks, that alien called Christopher told the doctor about in asylum of sound?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 21: The back-story behind the tragedy that led to the creation of the Daleks, as told by the alien Christopher in "Asylum of Sound," is that the Daleks were created from the remains of a group of human scientists who were trying to create a new form of life that could survive in the deserts of Mars. They failed, and the Daleks were created as a result of a mix-up in the laboratory, with the robots becoming hostile and turning on their creators.', "Doctor Who\nQuestion 22:  What did the Doctor think of the alien's behavior in asylum of sound?", 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 22: The Doctor was initially confused and angered by the alien\'s behavior in "Asylum of Sound," as he saw it as a violation of his rule against intervening in the lives of others. However, as he learned more about the alien\'s background and intentions, he came to see him as a being who was struggling with his own identity and feelings, and who was ultimately a product of his experiences and circumstances.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 23:  Who is the Doctor, portrayed by which actor?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 23: The Doctor is portrayed by various actors over the years, including William Hartnell, Patrick Troughton, Jon Pertwee, Tom Baker, Peter Davison, Colin Baker, Sylvester McCoy, Paul McGann, Christopher Eccleston, David Tennant, Matt Smith, and Jodie Whittaker.', 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 24:  Who is the companions of the doctor, called Rose, portrayed by which actor?', "Doctor Who\nAnswer 24: Rose Tyler, one of the Doctor's companions in the series, is portrayed by Doctor Who actress Billie Piper.", 'Doctor Who\nQuestion 25:  What are the names of the remaining two companions of the doctor?', 'Doctor Who\nAnswer 25: The names of the remaining two companions of the Doctor are Donna Noble, portrayed by Catherine Tate, and Martha Jones, portrayed by Freema Agyeman.', 'Doctor Who\nAll the incidental music for the 2005 revived series has been composed by Murray Gold and Ben Foster and has been performed by the BBC National Orchestra of Wales from the 2005 Christmas episode "The Christmas Invasion" onwards. A concert featuring the orchestra performing music from the first two series took place on 19 November 2006 to raise money for Children in Need. David Tennant hosted the event, introducing the different sections of the concert. Murray Gold and Russell T Davies answered questions during the interval and Daleks and Cybermen appeared whilst music from their stories was played. The concert aired on BBCi on Christmas Day 2006. A Doctor Who Prom was celebrated on 27 July 2008 in the Royal Albert Hall as part of the annual BBC Proms. The BBC Philharmonic and the London Philharmonic Choir performed Murray Gold\'s compositions for the series, conducted by Ben Foster, as well as a selection of classics based on the theme of space and time. The event was presented by Freema Agyeman and guest-presented by various other stars of the show with numerous monsters participating in the proceedings. It also featured the specially filmed mini-episode "Music of the Spheres", written by Russell T Davies and starring David Tennant.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'All the incidental music for the 2005 revived series has been composed by Murray Gold and Ben Foster and has been performed by the BBC National Orchestra of Wales from the 2005 Christmas episode "The Christmas Invasion" onwards. A concert featuring the orchestra performing music from the first two series took place on 19 November 2006 to raise money for Children in Need. David Tennant hosted the event, introducing the different sections of the concert. Murray Gold and Russell T Davies answered questions during the interval and Daleks and Cybermen appeared whilst music from their stories was played. The concert aired on BBCi on Christmas Day 2006. A Doctor Who Prom was celebrated on 27 July 2008 in the Royal Albert Hall as part of the annual BBC Proms. The BBC Philharmonic and the London Philharmonic Choir performed Murray Gold\'s compositions for the series, conducted by Ben Foster, as well as a selection of classics based on the theme of space and time. The event was presented by Freema Agyeman and guest-presented by various other stars of the show with numerous monsters participating in the proceedings. It also featured the specially filmed mini-episode "Music of the Spheres", written by Russell T Davies and starring David Tennant.', 'question': 'Topic: Doctor Who\nWho has performed all the Doctor Who music since the 2005 Christmas special?', 'answer': 'the BBC National Orchestra of Wales'}, {'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'All the incidental music for the 2005 revived series has been composed by Murray Gold and Ben Foster and has been performed by the BBC National Orchestra of Wales from the 2005 Christmas episode "The Christmas Invasion" onwards. A concert featuring the orchestra performing music from the first two series took place on 19 November 2006 to raise money for Children in Need. David Tennant hosted the event, introducing the different 